==Overview==
In ''The Last Airbender'', a fictional universe composed of four sovereign nations, some people are "benders" and can control air, fire, earth or water. Only the Avatar—a being who represents the bridge between the physical and spirit worlds—can master all four elements and thus bring balance to the world, but has been missing for the past 100 years.<ref name=speakeasy>{{cite web|last=Farley|first=Christopher John|title='The Last Airbender: Legend of Korra': The Creators Speak|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/BL-SEB-64190|work=The Wall Street Journal|date=March 8, 2011|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120524154510/http://blogs.wsj.com/speakeasy/2011/03/08/the-last-airbender-legend-of-korra-the-creators-speak/|archive-date=May 24, 2012|access-date=May 31, 2022|url-status=live}}</ref> During this absence, a one hundred year-long [[world war]] started by the Fire Nation resulted in the Air Nomads' [[genocide]], the near extinction of the Southern Tribes' waterbending population, and the Earth Kingdom's extensive [[forced displacement]] and [[colonization]].

===Character conception===
Character designs were originated from a series of drawings by one of the show's creators, [[Bryan Konietzko]]. The main sketch depicted a middle-aged [[monk]] with an arrow on his head and later included a flying bison as his pet. Konietzko's partner, [[Michael Dante DiMartino]], was interested in documentaries related to the [[South Pole]] at the time. They combined these ideas and created the concept of an "air guy" and "water guys" trapped in a snowy wasteland, with "fire guys" invading them.<ref name="NickMagInterview1">{{Cite journal|last=DiMartino|first=Michael Dante|author2=Konietzko, Bryan |title=In Their Elements|journal=Nickelodeon Magazine|page=6|year=2006|issue=Winter 2006}}</ref> Additionally, the writers based the characters' different bending abilities on distinct styles of [[martial arts]].

The characters of ''Avatar: The Last Airbender'' were designed by [[Michael Dante DiMartino]] and [[Bryan Konietzko]], the co-creators of the series.<ref name="NickMagInterview1"/> The anime-styled character art was inspired by [[Shinichiro Watanabe]]'s ''[[Cowboy Bebop]]'', ''[[Samurai Champloo]]'', and ''[[FLCL]]'' (''Fooly Cooly'') of [[Gainax]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ign.com/articles/2008/07/27/sdcc-08-avatar-creators-interview|title=SDCC 08: Avatar Creators Interview|website=IGN|author=Mell, Tory Ireland|date=July 26, 2008|access-date=May 31, 2022}}</ref> The original character conception was derived from a sketch by Bryan Konietzko that depicted a middle-aged balding man with an arrow on his head.<ref name="NickMagInterview1"/> Studios such as [[Studio 4°C]], [[Production I.G]], and [[Studio Ghibli]], which produced anime-styled cartoons, were also sources of inspiration.<ref>{{Cite journal|last =Mullins|first =Summer|title =Creation Station, an interview with Bryan Konietzko and Michael DiMartino|journal =Creation Station|issue =39|page =74}}</ref>

===Influences===
The greatest influences on the series were [[History of Eastern art|Asian art]], culture and history; the characters' various personalities and traits are based on philosophical teachings such as [[Taoism]] and [[Buddhism]].<ref>{{cite web| url = http://www.nicksplat.com/Whatsup/200510/12000135.html| archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20071217111256/http://www.nicksplat.com/Whatsup/200510/12000135.html| archive-date = December 17, 2007| title = Interview With The Creators| access-date = December 2, 2006|date=October 12, 2005| publisher = NickSplat.com}}</ref><ref name="nytimes">{{Cite news| url = https://www.nytimes.com/2005/08/28/arts/television/28lass.html| title = Kung Fu Fightin' Anime Stars, Bo| access-date = December 2, 2006| author = Mark Lasswell|date=August 25, 2005| newspaper=New York Times}}</ref> In the show, some characters have the ability to manipulate one of the four classic elements of ancient philosophy: [[Water (classical element)|Water]], [[Earth (classical element)|Earth]], [[Fire (classical element)|Fire]] and [[Air (classical element)|Air]], although the Avatar has the ability to control all four.<ref>{{cite web| url = http://www.musogato.com/avatar/calligraphy.html| title = Distant Horizon: Avatar Calligraphy| access-date = December 9, 2006}}</ref> Each of these employ a different form of [[martial arts]] in their fighting choreography: {{translit|zh|[[tai chi]]}} for [[Avatar: The Last Airbender#Elements|Waterbending]], [[Hung Gar]] for [[Avatar: The Last Airbender#Elements|Earthbending]], [[Northern Shaolin]] for [[Avatar: The Last Airbender#Elements|Firebending]], and {{translit|zh|[[baguazhang]]}} for [[Airbender#Elements|Airbending]].<ref name="avatar2/index.jhtml">{{cite web| url = http://www.nick.com/shows/avatar| title = Nickelodeon's Official Avatar: The Last Airbender Flash Site| access-date = December 2, 2006| publisher = Nickelodeon}}</ref> These individual styles of martial arts also reflect on the personalities of the user and the nations as a whole.<ref name="The Storm">{{cite episode|title=The Storm|airdate=June 3, 2005|season=1|number=12|credits=Director: Lauren MacMullan; Writer: Aaron Ehasz|series=Avatar: The Last Airbender|network=Nickelodeon|episode-link=Avatar: The Last Airbender (Season 1)#ep12}}</ref><ref name="Sozin Comet">{{cite web|url=https://animesuperhero.com/sozins-comet-produces-epic-season-finale-for-avatar-the-last-airbender/ |title="Sozin's Comet" Produces an Epic Season Finale for "Avatar the Last Airbender" |author=Liu, Ed |publisher=Anime Superhero|date=July 18, 2008 |access-date=May 31, 2022 |url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080722031445/http://news.toonzone.net/article.php?ID=25009 |archive-date=July 22, 2008 }}</ref><ref name="Kyoshi">{{cite episode|title=The Warriors of Kyoshi|airdate=March 4, 2005|season=1|number=4|credits=Director: Giancarlo Volpe; Writer: Nick Malis|series=Avatar: The Last Airbender|network=Nickelodeon|episode-link=Avatar: The Last Airbender (Season 1)#ep4}}</ref> These starkly individual tendencies are explained in eighty-five distinct types of "{{translit|zh|[[Neijia|jings]]}}{{-"}}, or internal energy.<ref name="Return To Omashu">{{cite episode|title=Return to Omashu|airdate=April 7, 2006|season=2|number=3|credits=Director: Ethan Spaulding; Writer: Elizabeth Welch Ehasz|series=Avatar: The Last Airbender|series-link=Avatar: The Last Airbender|network=Nickelodeon|episode-link=Avatar: The Last Airbender (Season 2)#ep23}}</ref> For example: {{translit|zh|baguazhang}} employs the "negative {{translit|zh|jing}}{{-"}} to create erratic circular movements and capitalizes on [[centripetal force]] and defensive positions; Northern Shaolin follows the "positive {{translit|zh|jing}}{{-"}} and emphasizes brute strength and aggression to generate power; and Hung Gar uses the "neutral {{translit|zh|jing}}{{-"}} in its ability to predict an opponent's movements and heavily relies on patience and reacting to the opponent's fighting accordingly.<ref name="avatar2/index.jhtml"/>

The cultures of the four nations are also greatly influenced by East Asian and [[Indigenous peoples|indigenous]] cultures. The Water Tribe is based on [[Inuit]], [[Yupik peoples|Yupik]], and [[Sirenik]] cultures; the Fire Nation on [[Imperial China]] and [[Imperial Japan]]; the Air Nomads on Tibetan Buddhist monks, [[Tibetan culture]], [[Buddhism]] and [[Hinduism]]; and the Earth Kingdom on [[Imperial China]] with Korean cultural influences.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.dailydot.com/parsec/legend-of-korra-avatar-last-air-bender-guide/|title=A beginner's guide to 'The Legend of Korra' and 'Avatar: The Last Airbender'|work=[[The Daily Dot]]|date=June 26, 2014|first=Michelle|last=Jaworski}}</ref>

===Personalities===
Many of the recurring characters of the series have received traits based on the respective element. Aang is carefree and childlike, as is commonly attributed to "wonder" of the unknown, or the "freedom" of the [[wind]] and the [[air]].<ref name="LostScrolls">{{Cite book|last=Mason|first=Tom|author2=Dan Danko |title=The Lost Scrolls: Air (Avatar: the Last Airbender)|publisher=Simon Spotlight/Nickelodeon|year=2006|isbn=1-4169-1879-5}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.scifi.com/sfw/anime/sfw12366.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080821233800/http://www.scifi.com/sfw/anime/sfw12366.html|title=Avatar: The Last Airbender|archive-date=August 21, 2008|access-date=February 15, 2008|last=Robinson|first=Tasha|date=March 7, 2006|publisher=[[Sci Fi Channel (United States)|Sci-Fi Weekly]]|page=2|url-status=dead}}</ref> As a spiritualist, a pacifist and a vegetarian, he is [[Yin and yang|very Yin]], hyper-attuned to the spirit world, and prefers non-violent alternatives to solving problems. Toph, despite being blind, is extremely perceptive of the world around her due to her connection to the Earth. Unlike Aang, she is extremely brusque when criticizing others, as attributed to Earth's toughness.<ref name="The Blind Bandit">{{cite episode|title=The Blind Bandit|airdate=May 5, 2006|season=2|number=6|credits=Director: Ethan Spaulding; Writer: Michael Dante DiMartino|series=Avatar: The Last Airbender|series-link=Avatar: The Last Airbender|network=Nickelodeon|episode-link=Avatar: The Last Airbender (Season 2)#ep26}}</ref><ref name="Bitter Work">{{cite episode|title=Bitter Work|airdate=June 2, 2006|season=2|number=9|credits=Director: Ethan Spaulding; Writer: Aaron Ehasz|series=Avatar: The Last Airbender|series-link=Avatar: The Last Airbender|network=Nickelodeon|episode-link=Avatar: The Last Airbender (Season 2)#ep29}}</ref>

