'''Sheev Palpatine''' ({{IPAc-en|'|p|ae|l|p|@|t|i:|n|audio=en-us-Palpatine.ogg}} {{respell|PAL|pə|teen}}) is a fictional character and the main [[antagonist]] of the ''[[Star Wars]]'' franchise. He first appeared in the 1980 film ''[[The Empire Strikes Back]]'' as {{No wrap|'''the Emperor'''}}. He is also known by his [[Sith]] name, '''Darth Sidious''', which was first used in [[Star Wars: Episode I – The Phantom Menace (novel)|the novelization]] of the 1999 film ''[[Star Wars: Episode I – The Phantom Menace|The Phantom Menace]]''.

Palpatine appears in all three film trilogies in the [[Skywalker Saga]], and is portrayed by [[Ian McDiarmid]] in all five films in which he physically appears.{{efn|name=eaton}} In the [[Star Wars original trilogy|original trilogy]], Palpatine is Emperor of the [[Galactic Empire (Star Wars)|Galactic Empire]] and the master of [[Darth Vader]]. In the [[Star Wars prequel trilogy|prequel trilogy]], which chronicles his rise to emperor, Palpatine is a senator from the planet [[Naboo]] who plots to become Supreme Chancellor of the [[Galactic Republic (Star Wars)|Galactic Republic]]. He masterminds the [[Clone Wars]] to turn the Republic into the Empire, destroys the [[Jedi|Jedi Order]], and manipulates [[Anakin Skywalker]] into becoming his apprentice, Darth Vader. In ''[[The Rise of Skywalker]]'' (2019), the final film in the [[Star Wars sequel trilogy|sequel trilogy]], a resurrected Palpatine is revealed to be the grandfather of [[Rey (Star Wars)|Rey]], a Jedi-in-training who is the protagonist of the sequel trilogy. He is also the mastermind behind [[Snoke]], whom he created to lead the [[First Order (Star Wars)|First Order]] against the [[New Republic (Star Wars)|New Republic]] and seduce Skywalker's nephew, Ben Solo, into becoming [[Kylo Ren]].

Palpatine's story was inspired by real-world examples of [[political strongman|political strongmen]] and [[democratic backsliding]] during the rise and rule of dictators such as [[Julius Caesar]], [[Napoleon Bonaparte]], and [[Adolf Hitler]]. Since the release of the original trilogy, Palpatine has become a widely recognized symbol of evil in popular culture. Since the prequel trilogy, he has been a symbol of sinister deception and the subversion of democracy. In addition to the films, Palpatine appears in the series ''[[Star Wars: Clone Wars|Clone Wars]]'', ''[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars|The Clone Wars]]'', ''[[Star Wars Rebels]]'', ''[[Star Wars: The Bad Batch|The Bad Batch]]'', ''[[Obi-Wan Kenobi (TV series)|Obi-Wan Kenobi]]'' and ''[[Star Wars: Tales]]''. He also appears in ''Star Wars'' novels and comics.

== Creation and development ==
===Concept and writing===
[[George Lucas]] created the ''Star Wars'' franchise and the character Palpatine. His conceptualization of Palpatine and the role he plays in the franchise changed over time. Lucas's initial notes discuss a line of corrupt emperors, not just one.{{sfn|Kaminski|2008|pp=170–172}} In the 1974 draft of [[Star Wars (film)|''Star Wars'']],{{Efn|Later retitled ''Star Wars: Episode IV—A New Hope''}} the "New Galactic Empire" was led by a relatively young human named Cos Dashit.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Keenan |first=Alex |date=2022-09-20 |title=George Lucas' Original Emperor Plan Would've Made Star Wars Worse |url=https://screenrant.com/star-wars-george-lucas-emperor-cos-dashit-bad/ |access-date=2023-05-12 |website=[[Screen Rant]] |language=en |archive-date=May 12, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230512013151/https://screenrant.com/star-wars-george-lucas-emperor-cos-dashit-bad/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The name Palpatine first appears in the prologue of [[Alan Dean Foster]]'s [[Star Wars: From the Adventures of Luke Skywalker|1976 novelization]] of ''Star Wars'', which details the Emperor's rise to power. From ''[[Return of the Jedi]]'' (1983) onwards, the Emperor became the ultimate personification of evil in the series. Though the films of the [[Original Star Wars trilogy|original ''Star Wars'' trilogy]] do not actually state the Emperor's name, [[Return of the Jedi (novel)|the novelization of ''Return of the Jedi'']] states his surname as Palpatine.<ref name="Kahn-1983">{{cite book |last=Kahn |first=James |title=Star Wars: Episode VI – Return of the Jedi |publisher=[[Del Rey Books]] |year=1983 |isbn=0-345-30767-4 |location=New York City |page=69}}</ref>{{sfn|Kaminski|2008|pp=170–172}} He was given the first name Sheev in the 2014 novel ''[[Star Wars: Tarkin|Tarkin]]''.<ref>{{cite book |last=Luceno |first=James |author-link=James Luceno |title=Tarkin |publisher=[[Del Rey Books]] |year=2014 |isbn=9780345511522 |location=New York City |page=93}}</ref>

During the story development of ''[[The Empire Strikes Back]]'' (1980), Lucas decided that, with the original having only passingly mentioned the Emperor, he needed to "begin to deal with him on a more concrete level".<ref>{{cite book |last=Bouzereau |first=Laurent |title=Star Wars: The Annotated Screenplays |publisher=[[Ballantine Books]] |year=1997 |isbn=978-0345409812 |location=New York City |page=173}}</ref><ref name="Rinzler-2010">{{Cite book |last=Rinzler |first=J. W. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=-U0PAAAAQBAJ&pg=PT36 |title=The Making of The Empire Strikes Back |publisher=[[Del Rey Books]] |year=2010 |isbn=978-1-84513-555-3 |location=New York City |pages=24 |oclc=506251987 |author-link=J. W. Rinzler |access-date=May 4, 2023 |archive-date=May 8, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230508045131/https://books.google.com/books?id=-U0PAAAAQBAJ&pg=PT36 |url-status=live }}</ref> Lucas realized, however, that the primary conflict with the Emperor would be best saved for the trilogy's third film, ''Return of the Jedi'', because "[w]hen you get rid of the Emperor, the whole thing is over." Discussing the character's importance to the story, Lucas stated that "In the end, the Emperor does exactly what [<nowiki/>[[Obi-Wan Kenobi]]] did; he can also transform himself", referring to the ability to [[Afterlife|live after death]] through [[the Force]].<ref name="Rinzler-2010" /> Although Palpatine dies at the end of ''Return of the Jedi'',<ref name="Star Wars Databank">{{cite web|title=Emperor Palpatine/Darth Sidious|url=https://www.starwars.com/databank/emperor-palpatine-darth-sidious|access-date=January 24, 2020|website=[[Star Wars Databank]]|archive-date=June 28, 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110628231042/http://www.starwars.com/databank/character/palpatine/?id=bts|url-status=live}}</ref> the plot point of his [[metamorphosis]] was utilized for the [[sequel trilogy]], which Lucas had no direct involvement with.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gemmill |first=Allie |date=2019-11-25 |title=Rise of Skywalker: J.J. Abrams Explains the Motive For Palpatine's Return |url=https://collider.com/j-j-abrams-says-palpatines-return-was-planned-since-the-force-awakens/ |access-date=May 3, 2023 |website=[[Collider (website)|Collider]] |language=en |archive-date=January 9, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200109122131/https://collider.com/j-j-abrams-says-palpatines-return-was-planned-since-the-force-awakens/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sciretta |first=Peter |date=2015-12-15 |title=Interview: J.J. Abrams Talks About Abandoning George Lucas' Treatments And Lessons Of The Star Wars Prequels |url=https://www.slashfilm.com/541461/jj-abrams-interview-star-wars-the-force-awakens/ |access-date=May 3, 2023 |website=[[/Film]] |language=en-US |archive-date=September 23, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240923034907/https://www.slashfilm.com/541461/jj-abrams-interview-star-wars-the-force-awakens/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In ''Return of the Jedi'', the initial conception of Palpatine as a weak, isolated figurehead<ref>{{cite web|first=Christopher|last=Gates|url=https://www.grunge.com/152098/false-facts-about-palpatine-you-always-thought-were-true/|title=False Facts About Palpatine You Always Thought Were True|website=[[Grunge.com]]|date=May 5, 2019|accessdate=February 7, 2024|archive-date=September 23, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240923034901/https://www.grunge.com/152098/false-facts-about-palpatine-you-always-thought-were-true/|url-status=live}}</ref> was superseded by his depiction as a dictatorial ruler adept in the dark side of the Force. The Emperor was inspired by the villain [[Ming the Merciless]] from the ''[[Flash Gordon]]'' comic books.<ref>{{cite book |last=Pollock |first=Dale |title=Skywalking: The Life and Films of George Lucas |date=1999 |publisher=Da Capo Press |isbn=978-0-306-80904-0 |location=New York City |page=142}}</ref> The characterization of Palpatine as a ruthless politician dismantling a [[democratic republic]] to achieve supreme power was in part inspired by the real-world examples of [[democratic backsliding]] during the rise and rule of [[Julius Caesar]], [[Napoleon Bonaparte]], [[Alberto Fujimori]], [[Ferdinand Marcos]], and [[Adolf Hitler]].<ref>{{cite book |last1=Reagin |first1=Nancy R. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=m81Bg4E_y_IC |title=Star Wars and History |last2=Liedl |first2=Janice |date=October 15, 2012 |publisher=[[Wiley (publisher)|Wiley]] |isbn=9781118285251 |location=Hoboken, New Jersey |pages=32, 144 |access-date=August 30, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230111154528/https://books.google.com/books?id=m81Bg4E_y_IC |archive-date=January 11, 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |date=April 21, 2002 |title=Star Wars: Attack of the Clones |url=http://www.time.com/time/covers/1101020429/story2.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20020605161308/http://www.time.com/time/covers/1101020429/story2.html |archive-date=June 5, 2002 |access-date=December 13, 2009 |magazine=[[Time Magazine|Time]]}}</ref>{{Sfn|Kaminski|2008|p=184}} Other elements of the character come from [[Richard Nixon]].<ref>{{cite book |last=McDowell |first=John C. |title=The Gospel according to Star Wars: Faith, Hope, and the Force |date=2007 |publisher=Westminster John Knox Press |isbn=978-1611648140 |location=Louisville, Kentucky |page=105}}</ref>{{efn|In his early drafts, Lucas used the plot point of a dictator staying in power with the support of the military. In his comment (made in the prequel trilogy era) Lucas attributed this to Nixon's supposed intention to defy the [[Twenty-second Amendment to the United States Constitution|22nd Amendment]],{{sfn|Kaminski|2008|p=95}} but the president resigned in his second term. In the [[Star Wars: Episode II – Attack of the Clones (novel)|novelization of ''Attack of the Clones'']], it is noted that Palpatine had manipulated the law to stay in office as Supreme Chancellor for several years past his original term limit.}} Lucas said that [[Presidency of Richard Nixon|Nixon's presidency]] "got me to thinking historically about how do democracies get turned into dictatorships. Because the democracies aren't overthrown; they're given away."<ref>{{cite news |last=Caro |first=Mark |date=May 18, 2005 |title='Star Wars' inadvertently hits too close to U.S.'s role |newspaper=[[Chicago Tribune]] |url=https://www.chicagotribune.com/2005/05/18/star-wars-inadvertently-hits-too-close-to-uss-role/ |url-status=live |access-date=November 12, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171113003206/http://articles.chicagotribune.com/2005-05-18/features/0505180309_1_star-wars-sith-palpatine |archive-date=November 13, 2017}}</ref> Lucas also said, "The whole point of the movies, the underlying element that makes the movies work, is that you, whether you go backwards or forwards, you start out in a democracy, and democracy turns into a dictatorship, and then the rebels make it back into a democracy."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Dykstra |first=Debbie |title=Interview: George Lucas lets Ian McDiarmid and Hayden Christensen get their Revenge in Episode III |url=https://www.syfy.com/scifi/sfw/issue421/interview.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060909135351/http://www.scifi.com/sfw/issue421/interview.html |archive-date=September 9, 2006 |website=SciFi.com}}</ref>{{Multiple image
