Lucas wanted to establish the Emperor as the true source of evil in ''Star Wars''. Screenwriter [[Lawrence Kasdan]] noted, "My sense of the relationship between Darth Vader and the Emperor is that the Emperor is much more powerful ... and that Vader is very much intimidated by him. Vader has dignity, but the Emperor in ''Jedi'' really has all the power."<ref>Bouzereau, p. 265.</ref> He explained that the climax of the film is a confrontation between Vader and his master. In the first scene that shows the Emperor, he arrives at the [[Second Death Star|Death Star]] and is greeted by a host of [[Imperial stormtrooper|stormtroopers]], technicians, and other personnel. Lucas states he wanted it to look like the [[military parade]]s on "[[International Workers' Day|May Day]] in [[Soviet Union|Russia]]."<ref>George Lucas, commentary, ''Star Wars: Episode VI – Return of the Jedi'', Special Edition (DVD, 20th Century Fox, 2004), disc 1.</ref>

Lucas fleshed out the Emperor in the [[prequel trilogy]]. According to Lucas, Palpatine's role in ''[[The Phantom Menace]]'' is to explain "how Anakin Skywalker [later Vader] came to be Palpatine's apprentice" and the events that sparked Palpatine's rise to [[totalitarian]] power after being a [[senate]] representative.<ref>{{cite book |last=Bowen |first=Jonathan L. |title=Anticipation: The Real-Life Story of Star Wars: Episode I-The Phantom Menace |date=2005 |publisher=[[iUniverse]] |isbn=978-0-595-34732-2 |location=Lincoln, Nebraska |page=4}}</ref> [[Star Wars: Episode I – The Phantom Menace (novel)|The film's novelization]] is the first time he is called Darth Sidious.<ref>Bowen, pp. 93–94.</ref> His home world, [[Naboo]], is also home to Anakin's romantic interest, [[Padmé Amidala]], who later becomes a senator of that planet.

In the Lucasfilm-authorized ''[[Star Wars and History]]'', Palpatine's consolidation of power is described as being similar to the first [[Roman emperor]], [[Augustus]]. Both legitimized [[authoritarian]] rule by saying that corruption in the Senate was hampering the powers of the head of state; both pressured the Senate to grant extraordinary powers to deal with a crisis, falsely claiming that they would rescind those powers once the crisis was over; and both relied on their strong control over military force.<ref>{{cite book |title=Star Wars and History |date=2012 |publisher=[[Wiley (publisher)|Wiley]] |isbn=978-0470602003 |editor1-last=Liedl |editor1-first=Janice |location=[[Hoboken, New Jersey]] |editor2-last=Reagin |editor2-first=Nancy R.}}</ref>

The director of ''[[The Rise of Skywalker]]'' (2019), [[J.&nbsp;J. Abrams]], stated of Palpatine's return from the dead that "when you look at this as nine chapters of a story, perhaps the weirder thing would be if Palpatine didn't return. You just look at what he talks about, who he is, how important he is, what the story is — strangely, his absence entirely from the third trilogy would be conspicuous".<ref>{{cite web |last=Ryan |first=Mike |date=November 25, 2019 |title=J.J. Abrams On ''The Rise Of Skywalker'' And The Return Of Palpatine (And, Yes, Maclunkey) |url=https://uproxx.com/movies/jj-abrams-interview-the-rise-of-skywalker-star-wars/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200109122131/https://uproxx.com/movies/jj-abrams-interview-the-rise-of-skywalker-star-wars/ |archive-date=January 9, 2020 |access-date=January 11, 2020 |website=[[Uproxx]] |publisher=Uproxx Media Group |location=Culver City, California}}</ref>{{efn|Although Lucas seeded the idea that Palpatine might return,<ref name="Rinzler-2010" /> while promoting ''[[The Force Awakens]]'', Abrams stated that the [[First Order (Star Wars)|revived empire]] was inspired by the idea that the [[Ratlines (World War II)|Nazis fled to South America]] and [[Fourth Reich|continued their schemes]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=August 26, 2015 |title='Star Wars: The Force Awakens': First Order Inspired by Nazis |url=https://abcnews.go.com/Entertainment/star-wars-force-awakens-order-inspired-nazis/story?id=33338629 |access-date=2025-12-23 |website=ABC News |language=en |quote=That all came out of conversations about what would have happened if the Nazis all went to Argentina but then started working together again? |others=J.&nbsp;J. Abrams}}</ref> Hitler, (an inspiration for Palpatine){{Sfn|Kaminski|2008|p=184}} has been similarly claimed to have [[Conspiracy theories about Adolf Hitler's death|somehow returned]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Pollina |first=Richard |date=2025-04-09 |title=CIA files reveal search for Hitler in South America 10 years after his suicide as Argentina prepares to release classified docs on Nazi fugitives |url=https://nypost.com/2025/04/09/us-news/cia-files-reveal-search-for-hitler-in-south-america-10-years-after-his-suicide-as-argentina-prepares-to-release-classified-docs-on-nazi-fugitives/ |access-date=2025-12-23 |website=[[New York Post]] |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last=Basti |first=Abel |title=Las fotos de Hitler después de la guerra |date=2023 |publisher=Planeta Colombia |language=es}} Contains reproduction of an [[Philip Citroën|alleged 1954 photograph]].</ref>}}{{efn|Although initially surprised to learn the character would return,<ref name="Schoellkopf-2019" /> Palpatine portrayer [[Ian McDiarmid]] defended the decision, stating in 2022 that "A&nbsp;lot of people said, 'You can't bring him back, he was dead! Did you see that fall? How could anyone survive that?' ... He's the Emperor ... Anyway, nobody's going to tell me he wouldn't have had a Plan&nbsp;B ... And of course he had the best operational ward team of surgeons."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Silverio |first=Ben F. |date=2022-05-27 |title=Ian McDiarmid Defends The Rise Of Skywalker's 'Somehow Palpatine Returned' Plot |url=https://www.slashfilm.com/876815/ian-mcdiarmid-defends-the-rise-of-skywalkers-somehow-palpatine-returned-plot/ |access-date=2025-05-26 |website=SlashFilm |language=en-US}}</ref>}}

