When the Emperor first appeared in ''The Empire Strikes Back'', he was portrayed by [[Marjorie Eaton]] under heavy makeup.<ref>{{cite web |last=Wilkens |first=Alasdair |date=October 10, 2010 |title=Yoda was originally played by a monkey in a mask, and other secrets of The Empire Strikes Back |url=http://io9.com/5660319/yoda-was-originally-played-by-a-monkey-in-a-mask-and-other-secrets-of-the-empire-strikes-back |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101017055944/http://io9.com/5660319/yoda-was-originally-played-by-a-monkey-in-a-mask-and-other-secrets-of-the-empire-strikes-back |archive-date=October 17, 2010 |access-date=October 10, 2010 |website=[[i09]] |publisher=[[Univision Communications]] |location=New York City}}</ref> [[Common chimpanzee|Chimpanzee]] eyes were superimposed into darkened eye sockets during post-production.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Reddish |first=David |date=2016-11-19 |title=Star Wars: 15 Things You Never Knew About Emperor Palpatine |url=https://screenrant.com/star-wars-emperor-palpatine-darth-sidious-facts-trivia/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210521082925/https://screenrant.com/star-wars-emperor-palpatine-darth-sidious-facts-trivia/ |archive-date=May 21, 2021 |access-date=2021-05-21 |website=Screen Rant |language=en-US}}</ref> The character was voiced by [[Clive Revill]].<ref>Palpatine, Behind the Scenes, at the [https://www.starwars.com/databank/character/palpatine/?id=bts Star Wars Databank]. Retrieved May 18, 2017. [https://web.archive.org/web/20061201010719/http://www.starwars.com/databank/character/palpatine/?id=bts Archived] December 1, 2006, at the [[Wayback Machine]].</ref> The makeup was sculpted by [[Phil Tippett]] and applied by [[Rick Baker (makeup artist)|Rick Baker]],<ref name="pablohidalgo-2016" /> who initially used his own wife, Elaine, for the makeup tests.<ref name="Gourley" /><ref>{{cite book |last=Rinzler |first=J.W. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=-U0PAAAAQBAJ |title=The Making of Star Wars: The Empire Strikes Back |date=October 22, 2013 |publisher=[[Ballantine Group]] |isbn=9780345543363 |edition=Enhanced |location=New York City |access-date=September 3, 2020 |archive-date=September 23, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240923034900/https://books.google.com/books?id=-U0PAAAAQBAJ |url-status=live }}</ref>

"With [[Irvin Kershner|[director Irvin] Kershner]]," Revill said, "you had to keep the reins tight — you couldn't go overboard. It was the perfect example of the old adage 'less is more' — the Emperor doesn't say very much. But when he finally appears, it's at a point in the saga when everyone's waiting to see him. It's the Emperor, the arch-villain of all time, and when he says there's a great disturbance in the Force, I mean, that's enough ''oomph''!"<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Chernoff |first=Scott |date=May–June 2000 |title=Clive Revill: Voice of the Emperor |url=http://www.wizardsandwarriors.org/show/articles/starwars.htm |url-status=live |magazine=Star Wars Insider |location=London, England |publisher=[[Titan Magazines]] |issue=60 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160409161125/http://www.wizardsandwarriors.org/show/articles/starwars.htm |archive-date=April 9, 2016 |access-date=January 8, 2018 |via=wizardsandwarriors.org}}</ref> Years later, during the production of ''[[Revenge of the Sith]]'' (2005), Lucas decided to shoot new footage for ''The Empire Strikes Back'' to create [[Continuity (fiction)|continuity]] between the prequels and original trilogy. Thus, in the 2004 DVD release of ''The Empire Strikes Back'' Special Edition, the original version of the Emperor was replaced by [[Ian McDiarmid]], and the dialogue between the Emperor and Darth Vader was revised.<ref>{{cite news |last=Hyde |first=Douglas |date=September 23, 2004 |title=Five major changes in the 'Star Wars' DVD |publisher=[[CNN]] |url=http://www.cnn.com/2004/SHOWBIZ/Movies/09/20/star.changes/index.html |url-status=live |access-date=August 17, 2006 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170303082041/http://www.cnn.com/2004/SHOWBIZ/Movies/09/20/star.changes/index.html |archive-date=March 3, 2017}}</ref>

[[File:Ian McDiarmid Photo Op GalaxyCon Richmond 2024.jpg|thumb|upright|From ''[[Return of the Jedi]]'' (1983), the role of the Emperor was portrayed by [[Ian McDiarmid]].]]
Lucas and director [[Richard Marquand]] cast McDiarmid to play Emperor Palpatine for ''Return of the Jedi''. He was in his late 30s and had never played a leading role in a feature film, though he had made minor appearances in films like ''[[Dragonslayer (1981 film)|Dragonslayer]]'' (1981). Due to the large age difference between McDiarmid and his character, Lucas and Marquand had initially intended to [[Dubbing|dub over]] McDiarmid, but reversed course after hearing McDiarmid's distinctive voice.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schedeen |first=Jesse |date=August 15, 2020 |title=Star Wars: How Sam Witwer Perfected Darth Maul's Voice |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/star-wars-return-of-the-jedi-wfh-theater-darth-maul-palpatine-voice |access-date=2025-04-27 |website=[[IGN]] |language=en}}</ref> After ''Return of the Jedi'', he resumed stage acting in London.<ref name="StarWars.com">{{cite web |title=Ian McDiarmid: Dark Force Rising |date=January 24, 2002|url=https://www.starwars.com/episode-ii/bts/profile/f20020124/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061112153958/http://www.starwars.com/episode-ii/bts/profile/f20020124/index.html |archive-date=November 12, 2006 |access-date=August 17, 2006 |website=[[StarWars.com]]}}</ref> In an interview with ''BackStage'', McDiarmid revealed that he "never had his sights set on a film career" and never auditioned for the role of Palpatine. He elaborated, "I got called in for the interview after a ''Return of the Jedi'' casting director saw me perform in the [[Sam Shepard]] play ''Seduced'' at a studio theatre at the [[Royal Court Theatre|Royal Court]]. I was playing a dying [[Howard Hughes]]."<ref>{{cite news |last=Horwitz |first=Simi |date=May 25, 2006 |title=The Emperor's New Role |work=BackStage |url=http://www.backstage.com/bso/esearch/article_display.jsp?vnu_content_id=1002577371 |url-status=live |access-date=September 5, 2006 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200209120533/https://www.backstage.com/magazine/article/emperors-new-role-43527/ |archive-date=February 9, 2020}}</ref>

Palpatine's role in the prequel films required McDiarmid to portray two aspects of the same character. Recalling the initial days of shooting ''The Phantom Menace'', McDiarmid stated, "Palpatine's an interesting character; he's conventional on the outside, but demonic on the inside — he's on the edge, trying to go beyond what's possible."<ref>{{cite web |title=Actors and Characters - Part II: ''Star Wars'' Episode I: Production Notes |url=https://www.starwars.com/episode-i/bts/production/f19990501/indexp5.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060530063902/http://www.starwars.com/episode-i/bts/production/f19990501/indexp5.html |archive-date=May 30, 2006 |access-date=August 17, 2006 |website=[[StarWars.com]]}}</ref> McDiarmid added another layer to the character in ''Attack of the Clones''. He said, "[Palpatine] is a supreme actor. He has to be even more convincing than somebody who isn't behaving in a [[schizophrenic]] fashion, so he's extra charming, or extra professional — and for those who are looking for clues, that's almost where you can see them." McDiarmid reflected on the scene in which Padmé Amidala is almost assassinated:

{{blockquote|There's a moment in one scene of the new film where tears almost appear in [Palpatine's] eye. These are [[crocodile tears]], but for all those in the movie, and perhaps watching the movie itself, they'll see he is apparently moved — and of course, he is. He can just do it. He can, as it were, turn it on. And I suppose for him, it's also a bit of a turn-on — the pure exercise of power is what he's all about. That's the only thing he's interested in and the only thing that can satisfy him — which makes him completely fascinating to play because it is an evil soul. He is more evil than the [[devil]].<ref>{{cite web|title=The Exercise of Power: Ian McDiarmid: Dark Force Rising|url=https://www.starwars.com/episode-ii/bts/profile/f20020124/indexp2.html|website=[[StarWars.com]]|date=January 24, 2002|access-date=August 17, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061215043150/http://www.starwars.com/episode-ii/bts/profile/f20020124/indexp2.html |archive-date=December 15, 2006 }}</ref>}}

In ''Revenge of the Sith'', McDiarmid plays an even darker version of the character. He explained that "when you're playing a character of solid blackness, that in itself is very interesting, in the sense that you have no other motivation other than the accumulation of power. It's not so much about not having a moral center, it's just that the only thing that mattered is increasing power."<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 26, 2005 |title=Palpatine's Point of View |url=https://www.starwars.com/episode-iii/bts/profile/beacon137.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061112122104/http://www.starwars.com/episode-iii/bts/profile/beacon137.html |archive-date=November 12, 2006 |website=StarWars.com}}</ref> McDiarmid asserted that everything Palpatine does "is an act of pure hypocrisy" and compared him to [[Iago]], the villain of [[William Shakespeare]]'s ''[[Othello]]''.<ref name="StarWars.com"/>

McDiarmid noticed that the script for ''Revenge of the Sith'' demanded more action from his character than in previous films. Lightsaber combat was a challenge to the 60-year-old actor, who, like his costars, took fencing lessons. The close-up shots and non-acrobatic sequences of the duel between Palpatine and Mace Windu were performed by McDiarmid.<ref>"Becoming Sidious," Web Documentary, ''Star Wars Episode III: Revenge of the Sith'', Bonus Materials (DVD, 20th Century Fox, 2005), disc 2; also available at [https://www.starwars.com/episode-iii/bts/me3/18.html StarWars.com]. Retrieved August 17, 2006. {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060807013911/http://www.starwars.com/episode-iii/bts/me3/18.html|date=August 7, 2006}}</ref> Advanced fencing and acrobatic stunts were executed by McDiarmid's stunt doubles.<ref>Cast and Crew of ''Revenge of the Sith'', at [https://www.starwars.com/episode-iii/castcrew.html StarWars.com]. Retrieved August 17, 2006. {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060815190336/http://www.starwars.com/episode-iii/castcrew.html|date=August 15, 2006}}</ref>

In ''The Rise of Skywalker'' (2019), McDiarmid returned to the role onscreen for the first time since ''Revenge of the Sith''.<ref name="Schoellkopf-2019" /> Ahead of the film's wide release, McDiarmid stated that Palpatine was "fairly physically impaired, but his mind is as sharp as ever".<ref name="Schoellkopf-2019" />

===Makeup and costumes===
Transforming McDiarmid into Emperor Palpatine for ''Return of the Jedi'' required extensive makeup, which took between two and four hours to apply.<ref name="StarWars.com-2006">{{cite web |date=December 15, 2006 |title=Ian McDiarmid: Dark Force Rising |url=https://www.starwars.com/episode-ii/bts/profile/f20020124/indexp3.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061215043157/http://www.starwars.com/episode-ii/bts/profile/f20020124/indexp3.html |archive-date=December 15, 2006 |access-date=April 24, 2019 |website=StarWars.com}}</ref> Film critic [[Roger Ebert]] wrote that the Emperor "looks uncannily like [[Personifications of death|Death]] in ''[[The Seventh Seal]]'',"<ref>{{cite news |last=Ebert |first=Roger |author-link=Roger Ebert |date=March 14, 1997 |title=Return of the Jedi, Special Edition |work=[[Chicago Sun-Times]] |publisher=[[Sun-Times Media Group]] |location=Chicago, Illinois |url=http://rogerebert.suntimes.com/apps/pbcs.dll/article?AID=%2F19970314%2FREVIEWS%2F703140303%2F1023 |url-status=live |access-date=August 17, 2006 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121010051801/http://rogerebert.suntimes.com/apps/pbcs.dll/article?AID=%2F19970314%2FREVIEWS%2F703140303%2F1023 |archive-date=October 10, 2012 |via=rogerebert.com}}</ref> and film historian [[Robin Wood (critic)|Robin Wood]] compared him to [[Evil Queen (Disney)|the hag]] from ''[[Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs (1937 film)|Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs]]'' (1937).<ref>{{cite book |last=Wood|first=Robin|url=https://archive.org/details/hollywoodfromvie00robi/page/154|title=Hollywood from Vietnam to Reagan...and Beyond: A Revised and Expanded Edition of the Classic Text|date=2003|publisher=[[Columbia University Press]]|isbn=978-0-231-12966-4|location=New York City|page=[https://archive.org/details/hollywoodfromvie00robi/page/154 154]|author-link=Robin Wood (critic)}}</ref> McDiarmid remarked, "When my face changes in [''Revenge of the Sith''], my mind went back to the early [[silent movie]] of ''[[The Phantom of the Opera (1925 film)|The Phantom of the Opera]]'' with [[Lon Chaney]]."<ref>"Palpatine's Point of View," in ''Homing Beacon'' 137, May 26, 2005, [https://www.starwars.com/episode-iii/bts/profile/beacon137.html StarWars.com]. Retrieved August 17, 2006. {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061112122104/http://www.starwars.com/episode-iii/bts/profile/beacon137.html|date=November 12, 2006}}</ref>

Palpatine's wardrobe in the prequels, tailored by costume designer [[Trisha Biggar]], played an important part in the development of the character. In ''Attack of the Clones'', explained McDiarmid, "The costumes ... have got much more edge to them, I think than the mere senator had in ''The Phantom Menace''. So we see the trappings of power."<ref name="StarWars.com-2006" /> McDiarmid's favorite costume in the film was a high-collared jacket that resembles reptile skin. He stated that "it just feels reptilian, which is exactly right for [Palpatine]." Biggar explained that the character's costumes become "progressively darker and more ornately decorated throughout the movie." She added: "He wears greys and browns, almost going to black, taking him toward the dark side."<ref>{{cite web |title=An Introduction to Episode III: Crafting ''Revenge'' |url=https://www.starwars.com/episode-iii/bts/production/f20050518/indexp3.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20051214182652/http://www.starwars.com/episode-iii/bts/production/f20050518/indexp3.html |archive-date=December 14, 2005 |access-date=August 17, 2006 |website=[[StarWars.com]] |publisher=[[Lucasfilm]] |location=San Francisco, California}}</ref>

In ''The Rise of Skywalker'', Palpatine is unable to move without the aid of a large machine, to which he is attached.<ref name="Gonzales-2019" /> His eyes have no pupils and his hands are rotting.<ref name="Gonzales-2019" /> Costume designer [[Michael Kaplan (costume designer)|Michael Kaplan]] opted to dress Palpatine in a utilitarian black robe, which he wears for the majority of the film.<ref name="Sporn-2019">{{cite magazine |last=Sporn |first=Stephanie |date=December 20, 2019 |title=How Michael Kaplan Costumed 700 Creatures in ''The Rise of Skywalker'' |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/how-michael-kaplan-costumed-700-creatures-rise-skywalker-1264308 |url-status=live |magazine=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |location=Los Angeles, California |publisher=[[Eldridge Industries]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200104011701/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/how-michael-kaplan-costumed-700-creatures-rise-skywalker-1264308 |archive-date=January 4, 2020 |access-date=January 11, 2020}}</ref> At the end of the film, Palpatine rejuvenates himself using the Force and becomes physically mobile.<ref name="Gonzales-2019" /> He dons a new costume—a formal robe with red velvet—which Kaplan refers to as "his true Emperor's garb".<ref name="Sporn-2019" />

=== Reception ===
[[Todd McCarthy]] of ''[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]'' described McDiarmid's portrayal of Palpatine in ''Revenge of the Sith'' as "dominant" and "worth writing home about".<ref>{{cite news |last=McCarthy |first=Todd |date=May 5, 2005 |title=Revenge of the Sith |work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |location=Los Angeles, California}}</ref> Reviewing the same film, [[Ed Halter]] of ''[[The Village Voice]]'' wrote that "McDiarmid's unctuous Emperor turns appropriately vampiric as he attempts to draw Anakin into the Sith fold with promises of eternal life."<ref>{{cite news |last=Halter |first=Ed |date=May 11, 2005 |title=May the Force Be Over; The end of the beginning: Lucas's adolescent space opera concludes in a CGI Sith Storm |work=[[The Village Voice]] |publisher=[[Voice Media Group]] |location=New York City |url=http://www.villagevoice.com/film/0520,halter1,63970,20.html |url-status=dead |access-date=August 17, 2006 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060628203757/http://www1.villagevoice.com/film/0520%2Chalter1%2C63970%2C20.html |archive-date=June 28, 2006}}</ref> McDiarmid was nominated for the [[Saturn Award for Best Supporting Actor]] for his performance in ''Revenge of the Sith.''{{Citation needed|date=June 2024}}

According to [[Cory Barlog]], the planned-but-never-produced ''[[Star Wars: Underworld]]'' television series would have depicted Palpatine in a sympathetic way.<ref>{{cite web |last=Moore |first=Trent |date=June 23, 2016 |title=Aborted Star Wars Underworld TV series would've featured a 'sympathetic' Emperor Palpatine |url=https://www.syfy.com/syfywire/aborted-star-wars-underworld-tv-series-wouldve-featured-sympathetic-emperor-palpatine |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190921091353/https://www.syfy.com/syfywire/aborted-star-wars-underworld-tv-series-wouldve-featured-sympathetic-emperor-palpatine |archive-date=September 21, 2019 |access-date=September 21, 2019 |website=[[Syfy|SyFy Wire]]}}</ref>

==Appearances==

===Film===
====Original trilogy ====
{{main|Star Wars original trilogy}}

The character is referred to as "the Emperor" in the [[Star Wars original trilogy|original trilogy]]. He is briefly mentioned in ''[[Star Wars (film)|Star Wars]]'' (1977), the first film in the original trilogy, which was later retitled ''Episode IV{{snd}}A New Hope''. On the [[Death Star]], [[Grand Moff Tarkin]] ([[Peter Cushing]]) explains to his fellow Imperials that the Emperor has dissolved the Imperial Senate, the last remnant of the [[Galactic Republic (Star Wars)|Old Republic]]. The Emperor does not appear on-screen, leaving Tarkin and [[Darth Vader]] (played by [[David Prowse]], voiced by [[James Earl Jones]]) as the film's main villains. The Emperor first appears in ''[[The Empire Strikes Back]]'', the 1980 sequel to the original film. He appears as a hologram to inform Vader that [[Luke Skywalker]] ([[Mark Hamill]]) has become a threat to the Empire. Vader persuades him that the young Jedi would be a great asset if he could be turned to the dark side of the Force.<ref name="Star Wars Databank-2">{{cite web|url=https://www.starwars.com/databank/character/palpatine/|title=Palpatine|website=[[Star Wars Databank]]|access-date=February 6, 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081216025044/http://www.starwars.com/databank/character/palpatine|archive-date=December 16, 2008|url-status=live}}</ref>

In 1983's ''[[Return of the Jedi]]'', the Emperor appears in person to oversee the last stages of the second [[Death Star]]'s construction. He assures Vader that they will together turn Luke, Vader's son, to the dark side of the Force. Unbeknownst to Vader, the Emperor plans to replace him with Luke; Vader, meanwhile, intends to overthrow the Emperor and rule the galaxy with Luke at his side. When Vader brings Luke before his master, the Emperor tempts Luke to join the dark side by appealing to the young Jedi's fear for his friends, whom he has lured into a trap.<ref name="Star Wars Databank-2"/> This leads to a [[lightsaber]] duel in which Luke defeats and nearly kills Vader. The Emperor urges Luke to kill Vader and take his place, but Luke refuses and declares himself a Jedi. Enraged, the Emperor attacks Luke with [[Force lightning]]. Unable to bear the sight of his son in pain, Vader throws the Emperor down a chasm to his death shortly before the Death Star is destroyed.<ref name="Star Wars Databank-2"/>

==== Prequel trilogy ====
{{main|Star Wars prequel trilogy}}

[[File:Palpatine1.jpg|left|thumb|[[Ian McDiarmid]] as Senator Palpatine in ''[[Star Wars: Episode I – The Phantom Menace|The Phantom Menace]]'' (1999)]]

In the 1999 prequel ''[[Star Wars: Episode I – The Phantom Menace|Episode I: The Phantom Menace]]'', set 32 years before ''A New Hope'', Palpatine (named onscreen for the first time) is depicted as a Galactic Senator from the planet [[Naboo]].<ref name="Star Wars Databank-2"/> As his [[alter ego]], the [[Sith]] Lord Darth Sidious, he advises the corrupt Trade Federation to blockade and invade Naboo.<ref name="Star Wars Databank-2"/> Queen [[Padmé Amidala]] of Naboo ([[Natalie Portman]]) flees to the planet [[Coruscant]] to receive counsel from Palpatine, unaware that he actually engineered the invasion. After a plea for help from the Senate results in [[bureaucracy|bureaucratic]] delays, Palpatine manipulates and deceives Padmé into calling for a [[motion of no confidence]] against [[Supreme Chancellor]] [[Finis Valorum]] ([[Terence Stamp]]).<ref name="20th Century Fox-1999">{{cite AV media|title=Star Wars Episode I: The Phantom Menace|medium=DVD|publisher=20th Century Fox|location=Los Angeles, California|year=1999}}</ref>

When Padmé attempts to liberate Naboo, Sidious sends his apprentice [[Darth Maul]] (portrayed by [[Ray Park]], voiced by [[Peter Serafinowicz]]) to capture her. The invasion is eventually thwarted, and Maul is defeated in a lightsaber duel with Jedi Padawan [[Obi-Wan Kenobi]] ([[Ewan McGregor]]).<ref name="Star Wars Databank-2"/> Palpatine uses the crisis to get elected as the new Chancellor of the Republic. He then returns to Naboo, where he befriends the nine-year-old Anakin Skywalker ([[Jake Lloyd]]), telling the boy, "We will watch your career with great interest."<ref name="20th Century Fox-1999"/>
[[File:Star Wars and the Power of Costume July 2018 35 (Darth Sidious' Senate chamber robes from Episode III).jpg|thumb|Senate costume of Darth Sidious in [[Star Wars: Episode III – Revenge of the Sith|Episode III]]]]

In the 2002 sequel ''[[Episode II: Attack of the Clones]]'', set 10 years later, Palpatine [[Continuismo|exploits constitutional loopholes to remain in office after his term expires.]] Meanwhile, as Sidious, he continues to manipulate events from behind the scenes by having his new Sith apprentice [[Count Dooku]] ([[Christopher Lee]]) lead a movement of planets in [[Secession|seceding]] to form the Confederacy of Independent Systems.<ref name="Star Wars Databank-2"/> He has the Separatists try to assassinate Padmé, now a senator, but the attempt on her life fails. He then arranges for Anakin (now played by [[Hayden Christensen]]) to guard Padmé on Naboo, which leads to them falling in love and marrying in secret.<ref name="20th Century Fox-2002">{{cite AV media|title=Star Wars Episode II: Attack of the Clones|medium=DVD|publisher=20th Century Fox|location=Los Angeles, California|year=2002}}</ref>

When Obi-Wan discovers that the Separatists are secretly building a [[battle droid]] army, Palpatine uses the situation to have himself granted [[State of emergency|emergency powers]].<ref name="Star Wars Databank-2"/> Palpatine feigns reluctance to accept this authority, promising to return it to the Senate once the crisis has ended. His first act is to allow a [[Human cloning|clone]] army's creation to counter the Separatist threat; this results in the first battle of the [[Clone Wars (Star Wars)|Clone Wars]]. With the galaxy now at war as Sidious planned, Dooku brings him the secret plans for the Death Star.<ref name="20th Century Fox-2002"/>

In the 2005 sequel ''[[Episode III: Revenge of the Sith]]'', set three years later, Palpatine is captured by [[cyborg]] Separatist leader [[General Grievous]] (voiced by [[Matthew Wood (sound editor)|Matthew Wood]]). Palpatine is rescued by Anakin and Obi-Wan, but not before the Jedi confront Dooku again. A duel ensues in which Anakin defeats Dooku. Palpatine then betrays Dooku by ordering Anakin to kill him; after a moment's hesitation, Anakin beheads Dooku in cold blood. Palpatine then escapes with his rescuers and returns to Coruscant. By this point, Palpatine has become a virtual dictator, able to take any action in the Senate. He makes Anakin his personal representative on the [[Jedi Council]], whose members deny Anakin the rank of Jedi Master and order him to spy on the Chancellor. Palpatine tells Anakin the story of [[Darth Plagueis]], a powerful Sith Lord who was able to create life and prevent death, but was killed by his own apprentice.{{efn|name=Plagueis|In the rough draft of ''Revenge of the Sith'', Palpatine tells Anakin that he "used the power of the Force to will the [[midichlorians]] to start the [[cell division]]s" that conceived him.<ref name="Young-2018"/>}} Eventually, Palpatine reveals his secret Sith identity to Anakin; he knows that Anakin has been having prophetic visions of Padmé dying in childbirth, and offers to teach him Plagueis' secrets to save Padmé's life.<ref name="20th Century Fox-2005">{{cite AV media|title=Star Wars Episode III: Revenge of the Sith|medium=DVD|publisher=20th Century Fox|location=Los Angeles, California|year=2005}}</ref>

Anakin informs Jedi Master [[Mace Windu]] ([[Samuel L. Jackson]]) of Palpatine's treachery. With three other Jedi Masters at his side, Windu attempts to arrest Palpatine, but Palpatine produces a lightsaber and dispatches all but Windu. Palpatine engages Windu in a duel and attacks him with Force lightning; Windu deflects the lightning back into Palpatine's face, deforming it into the gray, wizened visage first seen in the original trilogy. Before Windu can kill Palpatine, however, Anakin appears and, desperate to save Padme, betrays Windu, allowing Palpatine to kill Windu with another blast of lightning. Anakin then betrays the Jedi by pledging himself to the dark side as Palpatine's Sith apprentice, Darth Vader.<ref name="20th Century Fox-2005"/>

Palpatine issues [[Order 66 (Star Wars)|Order 66]], compelling the clone troopers to kill their Jedi generals for being "traitors" to the Republic,<ref name="Star Wars Databank-2"/> while dispatching Vader to kill everyone inside the Jedi Temple and then murder the Separatist leaders on the planet [[Mustafar]]. Palpatine then reorganizes the Republic into the Galactic Empire, with himself as Emperor, while framing the Jedi for treason.<ref name="Star Wars Databank-2"/> Jedi Master [[Yoda]] (voiced by [[Frank Oz]]) confronts Palpatine and engages him in a lightsaber duel that ends in a stalemate.<ref name="Star Wars Databank-2"/> Sensing that Vader is in danger, Palpatine travels to Mustafar and finds his new apprentice near death following a duel with Obi-Wan. After returning to Coruscant, he rebuilds Vader's burned, mutilated body with the black armored suit from the original trilogy. Palpatine then tells Vader that Padmé was killed in the heat of Vader's anger, breaking what remains of Vader's spirit. Palpatine is last seen watching the original Death Star's construction, with Vader and Wilhuff Tarkin ([[Wayne Pygram]]) at his side.<ref name="20th Century Fox-2005"/>

==== Sequel trilogy ====
{{main|Star Wars sequel trilogy}}

In the sequel trilogy, set three decades after the events of ''[[Return of the Jedi]]'', the [[First Order (Star Wars)|First Order]] has risen from the fallen Empire and seeks to destroy the [[New Republic (Star Wars)|New Republic]], the [[Resistance (Star Wars)|Resistance]], and Luke Skywalker. In the trilogy's first installment, ''[[The Force Awakens]]'' (2015), Palpatine's voice is heard during a vision that [[Rey (Star Wars)|Rey]] ([[Daisy Ridley]]) experiences upon touching Luke and Anakin's lightsaber.<ref>{{cite web|first=Kristen|last=Acuna|url=https://www.thisisinsider.com/star-wars-reys-vision-explained-2017-7|title=A new book reveals details about Rey's vision in 'Star Wars: The Force Awakens' you may have missed before|website=[[Insider Inc.]]|date=July 26, 2017|access-date=April 3, 2019|archive-date=April 4, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190404044009/https://www.thisisinsider.com/star-wars-reys-vision-explained-2017-7|url-status=live}}</ref> In the sequel, ''[[The Last Jedi]]'' (2017), Luke briefly mentions Palpatine as Darth Sidious while explaining the fall of the Jedi Order to Rey.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.slashfilm.com/history-of-palpatine-part-three/ |title=The Emperor Reborn: What Palpatine's Return Means For ''The Rise of Skywalker'' |website=[[/Film]] |date=November 7, 2019 |access-date=December 31, 2019 |first=Adam |last=Frazier |archive-date=November 8, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191108142500/https://www.slashfilm.com/history-of-palpatine-part-three/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Palpatine, again played by McDiarmid, appears in the trilogy's final film, ''[[The Rise of Skywalker]]'' (2019). Prior to the film's [[Star Wars opening crawl|opening]], Palpatine threatens revenge against the galaxy, having used the dark side to cheat death.<ref name="Star Wars Databank"/>{{efn|name=clone|While ''The Rise of Skywalker'' does not explicitly lay out how Palpatine returns, the film proposes an explanation: "dark science, cloning, secrets only the Sith knew".<ref name="Walt Disney Studios Motion Pictures-2019">{{cite AV media|title=[[Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker]]|medium=[[Blu-ray]]|publisher=[[Walt Disney Studios Motion Pictures]]|location=Los Angeles, California|year=2019}}</ref> Palpatine himself attributes his return to "unnatural" dark side abilities.<ref name="Robinson-2019"/> The film's [[The Rise of Skywalker#Tie-in literature and merchandise|novelization]] reveals that Palpatine had used [[Darth Plagueis]]' knowledge to cheat death by transferring his [[consciousness]] into a clone body.<ref name="Carson-2020">{{cite book|first=Rae|last=Carson|title=Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker Expanded Edition |publisher=[[Del Rey Books]]|location=New York City|date=2020|isbn=978-0593128404}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Fraser |first=Kevin |title=''Rise of Skywalker'' novel explains how the Emperor survived ''Return of the Jedi'' |url=https://www.joblo.com/movie-news/rise-of-skywalker-novel-explains-how-the-emperor-survived-return-of-the-jedi |website=JoBlo.com |access-date=March 4, 2020 |date=March 4, 2020 |archive-date=June 15, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200615075036/https://www.joblo.com/movie-news/rise-of-skywalker-novel-explains-how-the-emperor-survived-return-of-the-jedi |url-status=live }}</ref>}} This prompts First Order leader and fallen Jedi [[Kylo Ren]] ([[Adam Driver]])—the grandson of Anakin and nephew of Luke—to seek him out on the Sith planet [[Exegol]], where a physically impaired Palpatine is supported by machinery.<ref name="Schoellkopf-2019">{{cite news|first=Christina|last=Schoellkopf|url=https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/story/2019-12-17/star-wars-rise-of-skywalker-palpatine|title=Emperor Palpatine actor was done with ''Star Wars.'' Then J.J. Abrams called|newspaper=[[Los Angeles Times]]|date=December 17, 2019|access-date=December 18, 2019|archive-date=December 18, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191218003439/https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/story/2019-12-17/star-wars-rise-of-skywalker-palpatine|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Gonzales-2019">{{cite web |url=https://www.polygon.com/2019/12/19/21030344/how-is-emperor-palpatine-alive-in-star-wars-rise-of-skywalker-snoke-kylo-rey-vader-spoilers |title=Answering the biggest questions about Emperor Palpatine's return to ''Star Wars'' |website=[[Polygon (website)|Polygon]] |date=December 19, 2019 |access-date=January 11, 2020 |first=Dave |last=Gonzales |archive-date=December 21, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191221113548/https://www.polygon.com/2019/12/19/21030344/how-is-emperor-palpatine-alive-in-star-wars-rise-of-skywalker-snoke-kylo-rey-vader-spoilers |url-status=live }}</ref> Palpatine reveals himself as the power behind Ren's former master, [[Snoke]] ([[Andy Serkis]]), whom he created to rule the First Order and lure Ren to the dark side.<ref name="Robinson-2019">{{cite magazine |url=https://www.vanityfair.com/hollywood/2019/12/star-wars-rise-of-skywalker-easter-eggs-callbacks-references |title=A Guide to All the Old ''Star Wars'' References in ''The Rise of Skywalker'' |magazine=[[Vanity Fair (magazine)|Vanity Fair]] |date=December 20, 2019 |access-date=December 20, 2019 |first1=Joanna |last1=Robinson |first2=Anthony |last2=Breznican |author-link2=Anthony Breznican |archive-date=December 22, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191222145324/https://www.vanityfair.com/hollywood/2019/12/star-wars-rise-of-skywalker-easter-eggs-callbacks-references |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://screenrant.com/star-wars-rise-skywalker-first-order-reveals/ |title=''Star Wars: Rise of Skywalker'' Finally Explains How The First Order Are So Powerful |website=[[Screen Rant]] |date=December 25, 2019 |access-date=January 2, 2020 |first=Adrienne |last=Tyler |archive-date=December 31, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191231021007/https://screenrant.com/star-wars-rise-skywalker-first-order-reveals/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He then unveils the Final Order, a Sith armada of superlaser-equipped [[Star Destroyer]]s built by the [[Sith#Sith Eternal|Sith Eternal]]. Palpatine offers the Sith fleet to Ren on the condition that he find and kill the galaxy's last remaining Jedi, Rey, who is revealed to be Palpatine's granddaughter.<ref name="McCluskey-2019">{{cite web|first=Megan|last=McCluskey|url=https://time.com/5752170/who-are-reys-parents/|title=Breaking Down That Shocking Rey Reveal in ''Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker''|magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]|date=December 20, 2019|access-date=December 21, 2019|archive-date=December 22, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191222013348/https://time.com/5752170/who-are-reys-parents/|url-status=live}}</ref> It is subsequently revealed that Palpatine had a son who renounced him; the son and his partner took their daughter Rey to the planet [[Jakku]], assuming lives as "nobodies" to keep her safe. Palpatine's assassin Ochi eventually found Rey's parents and killed them on his orders, but never found Rey.<ref name="McCluskey-2019"/>

Near the end of the film, Rey arrives on Exegol to confront Palpatine. Surrounded by his Sith loyalists, Palpatine embodies all the Sith's power.<ref name="Star Wars Databank"/><ref name="Walt Disney Studios Motion Pictures-2019"/><ref>{{cite web |url=https://uk.ign.com/articles/2019/12/21/star-wars-rise-of-skywalker-exegol-sith-planet-wayfinder-explained |title=''Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker's'' Hidden Planet Explained |website=[[IGN]] |date=December 21, 2019 |access-date=December 25, 2019 |first=Jesse |last=Schedeen |archive-date=December 22, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191222220429/https://uk.ign.com/articles/2019/12/21/star-wars-rise-of-skywalker-exegol-sith-planet-wayfinder-explained |url-status=live }}</ref> He orders Rey to kill him in anger so his spirit can pass into her, which will allow him to [[Spirit possession|possess her body]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://screenrant.com/star-wars-rise-skywalker-palpatine-plan-sith-ritual/ |title=''Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker'' - Palpatine's Plan & Sith Ritual Explained |website=[[Screen Rant]] |date=December 26, 2019 |access-date=January 4, 2020 |first=Craig |last=Elvy |archive-date=December 27, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191227143717/https://screenrant.com/star-wars-rise-skywalker-palpatine-plan-sith-ritual/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Rey refuses, and she and Ren (now the redeemed [[Ben Solo]]) confront Palpatine together. Sensing their power as a [[Dyad (sociology)|dyad]] in the Force, Palpatine absorbs their life energy to rejuvenate his body. He incapacitates Ben and attacks the Resistance fleet with Force lightning. Rey uses the power of the past Jedi to face Palpatine once more; he attacks her with lightning, but Rey deflects it using the Skywalker lightsabers, killing Palpatine and destroying his Sith forces.<ref name="Walt Disney Studios Motion Pictures-2019"/><ref name="McCluskey-2019"/>

===Television ===

==== ''The Clone Wars'' ====
In the 2008 animated film ''[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars (film)|Star Wars: The Clone Wars]]'' and the [[Star Wars: The Clone Wars|subsequent animated series]] (set between ''Attack of the Clones'' and ''Revenge of the Sith''), Palpatine continues to serve as Supreme Chancellor while his Sith identity orchestrates the Clone Wars behind the scenes. Palpatine was voiced by [[Ian Abercrombie]] (from 2008 to his death in 2012), and by [[Tim Curry]] (from 2012 to 2014).<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2012/02/01/arts/television/ian-abercrombie-elaines-boss-on-seinfeld-dies-at-77.html |title=Ian Abercrombie, Who Played the Boss on ''Seinfeld'', Dies at 77 |newspaper=[[The New York Times]] |date=February 1, 2012 |access-date=January 18, 2020 |archive-date=January 23, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220123210451/https://www.nytimes.com/2012/02/01/arts/television/ian-abercrombie-elaines-boss-on-seinfeld-dies-at-77.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|first=Bryan|last=Young|url=https://www.huffpost.com/entry/tim-curry-star-wars_b_2776241|title=Exclusive: Tim Curry Joins ''Star Wars''|website=[[Huffington Post]]|date=February 28, 2013|access-date=January 18, 2020|archive-date=August 6, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806045715/https://www.huffpost.com/entry/tim-curry-star-wars_b_2776241|url-status=live}}</ref> In the film, Sidious engineers a Separatist plot in which Count Dooku (voiced by [[Corey Burton]]) turns [[Jabba the Hutt]] (voiced by [[Kevin Michael Richardson]]) against the Republic by kidnapping his son Rotta and framing the Jedi for it. Meanwhile, Palpatine suggests that the Republic ally itself with the Hutts. Although Anakin Skywalker (voiced by [[Mat Lucas]]) and his Padawan [[Ahsoka Tano]] (voiced by [[Ashley Eckstein]]) foil the plot, the outcome suits Palpatine's ends: Jabba places Hutt [[hyperspace (science fiction)|hyperspace]] routes at the Republic's disposal.<ref>{{cite AV media|title=[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars (film)|Star Wars: The Clone Wars]]|medium=DVD|publisher=[[Warner Bros. Pictures]]|year=2008}}</ref>

In season two of the TV series, Sidious hires bounty hunter [[Cad Bane]] (voiced by [[Corey Burton]]) to infiltrate the Jedi Temple and steal a [[holocron]]. He then takes a valuable Kyber memory crystal that contains the names of thousands of Force-sensitive younglings – the future of the Jedi Order – from around the galaxy. The final stage of the plot: to bring four Force-sensitive children to Sidious' secret facility on Mustafar. Anakin and Ahsoka again foil the plot, but Bane escapes and all evidence of Sidious' involvement is lost.<ref>{{cite AV media|title=[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars]] Season 2: Rise of the Bounty Hunters|medium=DVD|publisher=[[Warner Bros. Television]]|location=Los Angeles, California|year=2009}}</ref> In season three, Sidious senses Dooku's assassin [[Asajj Ventress]] (voiced by [[Nika Futterman]]) becoming powerful in the dark side and orders Dooku to eliminate her; he suspects that Dooku is planning to have Ventress assassinate him. Ventress survives and her revenge against Dooku sets off a chain of events including the return of Sidious' former apprentice and Dooku's predecessor, Darth Maul (voiced by [[Sam Witwer]]).<ref>{{cite AV media|title=[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars (2008 TV series)|Star Wars: The Clone Wars]] Season 3: Secrets Revealed|medium=DVD|publisher=[[Warner Bros. Television]]|location=Los Angeles, California|year=2010}}</ref>

In season five, Sidious personally travels to the planet [[Mandalore]] to confront Maul, who has become the leader of Death Watch. Sidious kills Maul's brother [[Savage Opress]] (voiced by [[Clancy Brown]]) before torturing Maul with the intent to make use of his former apprentice. In season six, Sidious goes to lengths to conceal his plan from the Jedi by silencing Clone Trooper Fives from learning of Order 66, and having Dooku wipe out anything tied to the former Jedi Master's [[Sifo-Dyas|connection]] to the conspiracy.<ref>{{cite AV media|title=[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars]] Season 5|medium=DVD|publisher=[[Warner Bros. Television]]|date=2012–13}}</ref><ref>{{cite AV media|title=[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars (2008 TV series)|Star Wars: The Clone Wars]] Season 6: The Lost Missions|medium=DVD|publisher=[[Lucasfilm]] Ltd.|location=Los Angeles, California|year=2014}}</ref>

In the seventh season episode "[[Shattered (Star Wars: The Clone Wars)|Shattered]]", Sidious issues Order 66.

==== ''Rebels'' ====
[[File:Sam_Witwer_by_Gage_Skidmore.jpg|thumb|[[Sam Witwer]] originally provided the voice for Palpatine in ''Rebels''.|left]]
In ''[[Star Wars Rebels]]'', set between ''Revenge of the Sith'' and ''A New Hope'', Palpatine is portrayed as the Emperor of the Galactic Empire. He briefly appears off-screen at the end of season two's premiere "[[List of Star Wars Rebels episodes#Season 2 (2015-16)|The Siege of Lothal]]" (originally voiced by [[Sam Witwer]] and later [[Ian McDiarmid]] from 2018). Darth Vader informs Palpatine that the [[Rebel Alliance]] cell on [[List of Star Wars planets and moons|Lothal]] has been broken and that Ahsoka Tano is alive and is now helping the Rebels. Palpatine sees this as an opportunity to seek out other remaining Jedi, ordering Vader to dispatch an Inquisitor to hunt down Ahsoka.<ref>{{cite AV media|title=Star Wars Rebels Season 2|medium=DVD|publisher=[[The Walt Disney Company|Walt Disney]]|year=2015}}</ref>

Palpatine returns physically in season four (voiced again by McDiarmid). In the episodes "Wolves and a Door" and "A World Between Worlds", he appears as a hologram overseeing the excavation of the Lothal [[Jedi Temple]], which contains a portal to a separate dimension of the Force outside of space and time, which Palpatine considers a 'conduit between the living and the dead' that could give him unrivaled power of the Force itself if he can access it. Shortly after [[Ezra Bridger]] (voiced by [[Taylor Gray (actor)|Taylor Gray]]) reaches through time and space to rescue Ahsoka from Vader, Palpatine sets up a portal that shows Jedi Master [[Kanan Jarrus]]' (voiced by [[Freddie Prinze Jr.]]) final moments. While Ezra wants to reach through the portal and rescue Kanan, Ahsoka convinces him not to. Palpatine then reveals himself through the portal and attacks Ezra with Force lightning. However, Ahsoka and Ezra manage to evade him and go their separate ways, thus denying Palpatine full power.<ref>{{cite AV media|title=Star Wars Rebels Season 4|medium=DVD|publisher=[[The Walt Disney Company|Walt Disney]]|location=Los Angeles, California|year=2017}}</ref>

Palpatine later returns in the [[series finale]] "Family Reunion - and Farewell". In the episode, Ezra, having surrendered himself to [[Grand Admiral Thrawn]] (voiced by [[Lars Mikkelsen]]) to protect Lothal, is taken by him to a room containing a reconstructed section of the ruined Jedi Temple and a hologram of Palpatine as he appears in the prequel films. Palpatine, having acknowledged the threat Ezra poses to the Empire, presents himself as a kindly old man and shows Ezra a vision of his dead parents through a doorway, promising that the youth will be with them if he enters it. Ezra is initially mesmerized by Palpatine's promise and goes to enter the door, but finally resists and destroys the reconstructed Jedi Temple and the illusion. Palpatine's hologram emerges from the rubble, flickering to show his true self, and commands his Royal Guards to kill Ezra, though Ezra manages to defeat them and escape. According to series creator [[Dave Filoni]], the events of ''[[Rogue One]]'' and ''A New Hope'' happen shortly after this episode, thus refocusing Palpatine's attention from Ezra and Lothal's liberation to the Rebel Alliance and Luke Skywalker.<ref>{{Cite web|first=Hanh|last=Nguyen|url=http://www.indiewire.com/2018/03/star-wars-rebels-finale-sabine-ezra-dave-filoni-1201936350/|title='Star Wars Rebels' Finale: That Surprise Ending Cameo, Ezra's Big Face-Off, and Possibilities for the Future|website=[[IndieWire]]|publisher=[[Penske Media Corporation]]|location=Los Angeles, California|date=March 6, 2018|access-date=June 23, 2018|language=en-US|archive-date=June 23, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180623193928/http://www.indiewire.com/2018/03/star-wars-rebels-finale-sabine-ezra-dave-filoni-1201936350/|url-status=live}}</ref>

==== ''The Bad Batch'' ====
Palpatine appears in the first season of the 2021 animated series ''[[Star Wars: The Bad Batch]]''. The series is set during (and immediately following) the events of the 2005 film ''Revenge of the Sith''. The series' first episode depicts Palpatine's Senate speech from the film in animated form, using the archive sound of McDiarmid's dialogue. As in the film, Palpatine orders the extermination of the Jedi and declares himself Emperor.<ref>{{cite web |first=David|last=Miller|url=https://screenrant.com/bad-batch-episode-1-revenge-sith-footage-reused/ |title=Every ''Revenge of the Sith'' Scene in ''Bad Batch'' Episode 1 |website=[[Screen Rant]] |date=May 6, 2021 |accessdate=May 8, 2021 |archive-date=May 8, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210508090746/https://screenrant.com/bad-batch-episode-1-revenge-sith-footage-reused/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Palpatine appears in season 3, in which he inspects the progress of Project Necromancer's clone experiments under the supervision of Imperial scientist Dr. Hemlock (voiced by [[Jimmi Simpson]]).<ref>{{cite web |first=Kevin |last=Erdmann |url=https://screenrant.com/star-wars-project-necromancer-palpatine-resurrection-explained/ |title=Star Wars' Project Necromancer & Palpatine's Resurrection Fully Explained |website=[[Screen Rant]] |date=25 February 2024 |access-date=February 25, 2024 |archive-date=September 23, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240923034919/https://screenrant.com/star-wars-project-necromancer-palpatine-resurrection-explained/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

==== ''Obi-Wan Kenobi'' ====
Palpatine appears in the [[Part VI (Obi-Wan Kenobi)|sixth episode]] of the 2022 series ''[[Obi-Wan Kenobi (TV series)|Obi-Wan Kenobi]]''. The series is set ten years after ''Revenge of the Sith''. After Darth Vader is defeated by Obi-Wan Kenobi following their second duel, Palpatine contacts Vader in his Mustafar castle via hologram, questioning Vader's motives and loyalty when Vader reports that he will not rest until Obi-Wan is found. Vader reassures Palpatine that Obi-Wan does not mean anything, reaffirms his commitment to Palpatine and abandons his search.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Outlaw |first=Kofi |date=June 22, 2022 |title=Obi-Wan Kenobi Finale Features Some Major Star Wars Character Cameos |url=https://comicbook.com/starwars/news/obi-wan-kenobi-qui-gon-jinn-liam-neeson-finale-spoilers-cameos/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220622115849/https://comicbook.com/starwars/news/obi-wan-kenobi-qui-gon-jinn-liam-neeson-finale-spoilers-cameos/ |archive-date=June 22, 2022 |access-date=June 22, 2022 |website=[[ComicBook.com]]}}</ref>

==== ''Tales of the Jedi'' ====
Darth Sidious appears in the fourth episode of the [[Tales of the Jedi (TV series)|''Tales of the Jedi'' TV series]], once again voiced by McDiarmid, talking with Count Dooku (voiced by [[Corey Burton]]).

==== ''Andor'' ====
While Palpatine does not appear in ''[[Andor]]'', he is regularly mentioned in the series, as the plot is largely driven by his political machinations and the formal organization of the Rebel Alliance to counter his ever-growing power in the five years leading up to the [[Battle of Yavin]]. After the heist on the Imperial garrison on [[List of Star Wars planets and moons#Aldhani|Aldhani]] masterminded by [[Luthen Rael]] ([[Stellan Skarsgård]]) in the [[The Eye (Andor)|sixth episode]], Palpatine's mandate to expand Imperial authority and intensify criminal penalties is conveyed to the Imperial Security Bureau (and the audience) in the [[Announcement (Andor)|following episode]] by Wullf Yularen ([[Malcolm Sinclair (actor)|Malcolm Sinclair]]).

One year later in 4 BBY in the [[One Year Later (Andor)|first episode]] of the second season, [[Orson Krennic]] ([[Ben Mendelsohn]]) heads a project conceived by Palpatine ostensibly to provide sustainable energy to the galaxy (later revealed to be a front for the Death Star), culminating in a massacre of protesters on the planet [[List of Star Wars planets and moons#Ghorman|Ghorman]] in the [[Who Are You? (Andor)|eighth episode]]. In the [[Welcome to the Rebellion|ninth episode]], Palpatine schedules an emergency Senate session but provides instructions to prevent dissident Senators from speaking. This backfires when Senator [[Bail Organa]] ([[Benjamin Bratt]]) invokes a rule written by Palpatine's own Council allowing him to yield his full time to [[Mon Mothma]] ([[Genevieve O'Reilly]]), who condemns the Ghorman Massacre as "unprovoked genocide" and denounces Palpatine as a "monster" before evading arrest with the aid of Luthen's spies.

Days before the Battles of Scarif and Yavin in the final three-episode block beginning with "[[Make It Stop (Andor)|Make It Stop]]" and ending with the series finale "[[Jedha, Kyber, Erso]]", Luthen learns from his spy within the ISB about the Death Star and its centrality to everything enacted by Palpatine or on his behalf up to that point in the series. Major Lio Partagaz ([[Anton Lesser]]) commits suicide rather than face Palpatine for his failure in capturing Luthen's assistant Kleya Marki ([[Elizabeth Dulau]]) before she can pass this information to the Rebel Alliance, leading directly into the events of ''Rogue One''.

=== Books and comics ===
The first appearance of Palpatine in ''Star Wars'' literature was in the prologue of [[Alan Dean Foster]]'s ghostwritten novelization of the script of ''A New Hope'', published as ''[[Star Wars: From the Adventures of Luke Skywalker]]'' (1976).<ref name="Lucas-1976">{{cite book|first1=George|last1=Lucas|author-link1=George Lucas|first2=Alan Dean|last2=Foster|author-link2=Alan Dean Foster|title=Star Wars: From the Adventures of Luke Skywalker|medium=paperback|publisher=[[Del Ray Books]]|location=New York City|date=1976|page=1|isbn=978-0-345-26079-6}}</ref>{{sfn|Kaminski|2008|p=172}} His background as a senator of the Republic was explored in [[James Kahn]]'s [[Return of the Jedi (novel)|novelization of ''Return of the Jedi'']].<ref name="Kahn-1983"/>

Palpatine also appears in [[Rae Carson]]'s [[The Rise of Skywalker#Tie-in literature and merchandise|novelization of ''The Rise of Skywalker'']], which expands upon the film's story. In the book, Palpatine is revealed to have discovered the "secret to immortality" from his former master, Darth Plagueis, using this knowledge to survive after his death in ''Return of the Jedi''.<ref name="Carson-2020" /><ref>{{cite web |url=https://comicbook.com/starwars/2020/03/04/star-wars-the-rise-of-skywalker-emperor-palpatine-return-darth-plagueis/ |title=''Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker'' Novelization Details How Palpatine Survived the Death Star II, Thanks to Plagueis |website=Comicbook.com |date=March 4, 2020 |access-date=March 21, 2020 |first=Patrick |last=Cavanaugh |archive-date=March 21, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200321091339/https://comicbook.com/starwars/2020/03/04/star-wars-the-rise-of-skywalker-emperor-palpatine-return-darth-plagueis/ |url-status=live }}</ref>{{efn|name=clone}} The novelization also describes Palpatine's son as a failed clone of himself.<ref name="Carson-2020" /><ref>{{cite web |last=Agar |first=Chris |title=''Star Wars'' Confirms Rey's Father Is A Failed Palpatine Clone |url=https://screenrant.com/star-wars-rise-skywalker-rey-father-palpatine-clone/ |website=[[Screen Rant]] |publisher=Valnet Inc. |location=Montreal, Quebec, Canada |access-date=March 4, 2020 |date=March 4, 2020 |archive-date=March 5, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200305053823/https://screenrant.com/star-wars-rise-skywalker-rey-father-palpatine-clone/ |url-status=live }}</ref> However, the 2021 book ''Skywalker: A Family at War'' describes Palpatine's son as an "offshoot of [his] genetic research, not precisely a clone but made of cloned tissue and donated cells."<ref>{{cite web |url=https://io9.gizmodo.com/star-wars-skywalker-family-history-uncovers-ben-solo-an-1846619340 |title=Star Wars' Skywalker Family History Uncovers Ben Solo and Rey's Childhoods |website=[[i09]] |date=April 5, 2021 |accessdate=April 7, 2021 |first=Germain |last=Lussier |archive-date=April 6, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210406005803/https://io9.gizmodo.com/star-wars-skywalker-family-history-uncovers-ben-solo-an-1846619340 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite book|first=Kristin|last=Baver|title=Star Wars Skywalker: A Family at War|publisher=[[DK (publisher)|DK]]|location=London, England|date=2021|isbn=978-0241467763}}</ref>

''[[Star Wars: Lords of the Sith]]'' (2015) was one of the first canon spin-off novels to be released in the Disney canon begun in 2014.<ref>{{cite web |date=April 25, 2014 |title=Disney and Random House announce relaunch of ''Star Wars'' Adult Fiction line |url=https://www.starwars.com/news/disney-publishing-worldwide-and-random-house-announce-relaunch-of-star-wars-adult-fiction-line |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160514073722/http://www.starwars.com/news/disney-publishing-worldwide-and-random-house-announce-relaunch-of-star-wars-adult-fiction-line |archive-date=May 14, 2016 |access-date=May 26, 2016 |publisher=StarWars.com}}</ref> In it, Vader and Palpatine find themselves hunted by revolutionaries on the [[Twi'lek]] home planet [[Ryloth]].<ref>{{cite web |first=Sean |last=Keane |url=http://www.nydailynews.com/blogs/pageviews/review-star-wars-lords-sith-blog-entry-1.2201665 |title=REVIEW: ''Star Wars: Lords of the Sith'' throws Darth Vader and the Emperor onto the battlefield |newspaper=[[Daily News (New York)|Daily News]] |location=New York City |publisher=[[Tribune Publishing]] |date=April 28, 2015 |access-date=May 27, 2016 |archive-date=August 8, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160808160504/http://www.nydailynews.com/blogs/pageviews/review-star-wars-lords-sith-blog-entry-1.2201665 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |first=Eric |last=Goldman |url=http://www.ign.com/articles/2015/05/09/star-wars-lords-of-the-sith-review |title=''Star Wars: Lords of the Sith'' Review |website=[[IGN]] |publisher=[[j2 Global]] |location=San Francisco, California |date=May 9, 2015 |access-date=May 27, 2016 |archive-date=June 6, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160606044616/http://www.ign.com/articles/2015/05/09/star-wars-lords-of-the-sith-review |url-status=live }}</ref> In ''[[Star Wars: Thrawn|Thrawn]]'' (2017), the [[Grand Admiral Thrawn|titular character]] warns Palpatine of "threats lurking in the Unknown Regions." [[Chuck Wendig]]'s ''[[Star Wars: Aftermath|Aftermath]]'' book trilogy reveals that, prior to his death, Palpatine enacted a plan for the remnants of the Empire, intended to be led by his adoptive son [[Gallius Rax]], to retreat to the Unknown Regions, where they formed into the [[First Order (Star Wars)|First Order]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Ratcliffe |first=Amy |title=The Possibilities of the Unknown Regions in STAR WARS |url=https://archive.nerdist.com/star-wars-unknown-regions/ |website=[[Nerdist]] |access-date=April 26, 2019 |date=June 2, 2017 |archive-date=April 26, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190426073347/https://nerdist.com/article/star-wars-unknown-regions/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The dark side was thought to be concentrated in this region, where one Sith cultist believed that Palpatine would be found alive.<ref>{{cite web |first=Stephen M. |last=Colbert |title=Star Wars 9 Theory: Snoke Was Actually Palpatine All Along |url=https://screenrant.com/star-wars-9-snoke-palpatine-true-identity/ |website=[[Screen Rant]] |publisher=Valnet, Inc. |location=Montreal, Ontario, Canada |access-date=April 26, 2019 |date=April 25, 2019 |archive-date=April 26, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190426073352/https://screenrant.com/star-wars-9-snoke-palpatine-true-identity/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The 2021 illustrated book ''[[Star Wars: The Secrets of the Sith]]'' is told from Palpatine's perspective.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Brooks|first=Dan|date=July 1, 2021|title=Unlimited Power! How Star Wars: The Secrets of the Sith Will Take Fans to the Dark Side|url=https://www.starwars.com/news/unlimited-power-how-star-wars-the-secrets-of-the-sith-will-take-fans-to-the-dark-side|url-status=live|access-date=July 3, 2021|website=[[StarWars.com]]|language=en-US|archive-date=July 1, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210701200113/https://www.starwars.com/news/unlimited-power-how-star-wars-the-secrets-of-the-sith-will-take-fans-to-the-dark-side}}</ref>

Palpatine appears frequently in the comic book series ''[[Darth Vader: Dark Lord of the Sith]]'' (2017–2018), written by Kieron Gillen and Charles Soule. It is suggested at the end of the series that Palpatine manipulated the Force to impregnate Vader's mother [[Shmi Skywalker]], making him, in essence, Vader's father—although this is left somewhat ambiguous.<ref name="Young-2018">{{cite web|url=https://www.slashfilm.com/darth-vaders-father-revealed/|title=Darth Vader's Father Revealed in Star Wars Comic?|last=Young|first=Bryan|date=December 28, 2018|website=[[/Film]]|access-date=December 30, 2018|archive-date=November 8, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201108000800/https://www.slashfilm.com/darth-vaders-father-revealed/|url-status=live}}</ref> This builds on the plot point of Anakin's [[Miraculous births|virgin birth]] introduced in ''The Phantom Menace'', and the claim that a Sith lord "could use the Force to influence the [[midi-chlorians]] to create life," as Palpatine tells Anakin in ''Revenge of the Sith''.{{efn|name=Plagueis}} This would seem to have vaguely [[incest]]uous implications for Rey and Ben Solo at the end of ''The Rise of Skywalker'',<ref>{{cite web |last=Gramuglia |first=Anthony |title=Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker Might Have More Accidental Incest |url=https://www.cbr.com/star-wars-the-rise-of-skywalker-accidental-incest/ |website=[[Comic Book Resources|CBR]] |publisher=Valnet, Inc. |location=Montreal, Quebec, Canada |access-date=December 26, 2019 |date=December 25, 2019 |archive-date=December 25, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191225212928/https://www.cbr.com/star-wars-the-rise-of-skywalker-accidental-incest/ |url-status=live }}</ref>{{efn|According to the film's novelization, their kiss was one of "gratitude, acknowledgement of their connection, celebration that they'd found each other".<ref>{{cite web |last1=Tassi |first1=Paul |title=No, The 'Star Wars: Rise Of Skywalker' Novel Didn't Kill The Kylo-Rey Romance |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/paultassi/2020/03/05/no-the-star-wars-rise-of-skywalker-novel-didnt-kill-the-kylo-rey-romance/ |website=[[Forbes]] |location=New York City |access-date=18 May 2020 |date=March 5, 2020 |archive-date=July 4, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200704232354/https://www.forbes.com/sites/paultassi/2020/03/05/no-the-star-wars-rise-of-skywalker-novel-didnt-kill-the-kylo-rey-romance/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}} but Soule says that "The Dark Side is not a reliable narrator," and a [[Lucasfilm]] story group member who collaborated on the comic confirmed that a direct connection between Palpatine and Vader was not their intent.<ref>{{cite web |last=Lovett |first=Jamie |title=Star Wars Story Group Member Debunks Palpatine and Anakin Theory |url=https://comicbook.com/starwars/2019/12/24/star-wars-darth-vader-emperor-palpatine-father-darth-plagueis/ |website=ComicBook.com |access-date=December 25, 2019 |date=December 24, 2019 |archive-date=December 24, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191224184128/https://comicbook.com/starwars/2019/12/24/star-wars-darth-vader-emperor-palpatine-father-darth-plagueis/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

The character also appears in the final chapter of the comic book ''Star Wars: The Rise of Kylo Ren'' (2020), which illustrates Palpatine's manipulation of the young Ben Solo into becoming Kylo Ren.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://comicbook.com/starwars/2020/03/11/star-wars-rise-kylo-ren-4-ben-solo-palpatine-influence-snoke-connections/ |title=''Star Wars'' Reveals How Palpatine Turned Ben Solo into Kylo Ren |website=Comicbook.com |date=March 11, 2020 |access-date=March 21, 2020 |first=Kofi |last=Outlaw |archive-date=March 21, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200321101827/https://comicbook.com/starwars/2020/03/11/star-wars-rise-kylo-ren-4-ben-solo-palpatine-influence-snoke-connections/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Video games ===
''[[Star Wars Battlefront II (2017 video game)|Star Wars Battlefront II]]'' adds a canonical tale spanning the destruction of the second Death Star through the events of ''[[The Force Awakens]]''. The story takes an Imperial perspective, following an elite squadron known as Inferno Squad, led by [[Iden Versio]], as they help execute ''Operation: Cinder'' following the Emperor's death. ''Operation: Cinder'' was the ultimate negative sum move carried out by the Galactic Empire as a means of devastating several Imperial planets a few weeks after the Battle of Endor. The operation was part of the "Contingency", a plan devised by Emperor Palpatine to ensure that the Empire and its enemies would not outlive him should he perish. It is the logic of "if I cannot have it, no one can"; echoing Hitler's [[Nero Decree]] in 1945 where he ordered the destruction of Germany's own infrastructure. He preferred to see his own country reduced to ash rather than surrender. The plan was put into action following the Emperor's death during the [[Battle of Endor]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://screenrant.com/star-wars-palpatine-plan-after-death/ |title=''Star Wars'': Palpatine's Plan For After His Death Revealed |website=[[Screen Rant]] |publisher=Valnet Inc. |location=Montreal, Quebec, Canada |date=June 23, 2019 |access-date=January 18, 2020 |first=Thomas |last=Bacon |archive-date=December 22, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191222075643/https://screenrant.com/star-wars-palpatine-plan-after-death/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Palpatine's threat of revenge referenced in the [[Star Wars opening crawl|opening crawl]] of ''The Rise of Skywalker'' was included in a [[Fortnite seasonal events#Chapter 2|live event]] in the video game ''[[Fortnite Battle Royale]]'' before the film's release.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://screenrant.com/star-wars-rise-skywalker-emperor-palpatine-message-fortnite/ |title=Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker'' Explains Missing Plot Point... In ''Fortnite |website=[[Screen Rant]] |publisher=Valnet Inc. |date=December 24, 2019 |access-date=February 19, 2020 |first=Thomas |last=Bacon |archive-date=February 19, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200219083929/https://screenrant.com/star-wars-rise-skywalker-emperor-palpatine-message-fortnite/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Palpatine was later added to ''Fortnite'' as a purchasable cosmetic outfit, as part of the [[Battle Pass]] for the ''Star Wars: Galactic Battle'' season.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.starwars.com/news/swcj-2025-fortnite-star-wars-galactic-battle |title=Fortnite & Star Wars Collide with Watch Party Island and Galactic Battle Season |website=Star Wars |date=April 20, 2025 |access-date=May 10, 2020 |first=Lyman |last=Paige }}</ref> The character has appeared in every [[Lego Star Wars video games|Lego Star Wars video game]] to date, including ''[[Lego Star Wars: The Skywalker Saga]].''<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.starwars.com/news/opening-night-live-lego-star-wars-the-skywalker-saga-trailer |title=Opening Night Live: 20 Details We Love From the New LEGO Star Wars: The Skywalker Saga Trailer |website=StarWars.com |date=August 27, 2020 |access-date=August 29, 2020 |archive-date=August 27, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200827213814/https://www.starwars.com/news/opening-night-live-lego-star-wars-the-skywalker-saga-trailer |url-status=live }}</ref>

== ''Star Wars'' Legends ==
{{See also|Star Wars in other media}}

Following the acquisition of [[Lucasfilm]] by [[The Walt Disney Company]] in 2012, most of the licensed ''Star Wars'' Expanded Universe material produced between 1977 and 2014 was rebranded as {{'}}''Star Wars'' Legends' and declared non-canonical.<ref>{{cite web |date=April 25, 2014 |title=The Legendary ''Star Wars'' Expanded Universe Turns a New Page |url=https://www.starwars.com/news/the-legendary-star-wars-expanded-universe-turns-a-new-page |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160910044317/http://www.starwars.com/news/the-legendary-star-wars-expanded-universe-turns-a-new-page |archive-date=September 10, 2016 |access-date=May 26, 2016 |website=[[StarWars.com]]}}</ref>

=== ''Clone Wars'' ===
Palpatine appears in the ''[[Star Wars: Clone Wars|Clone Wars]]'' micro-series, which is set between ''Attack of the Clones'' and ''Revenge of the Sith''. In the first chapter, Palpatine discovers that the InterGalactic Banking Clan has established battle droid factories on the planet [[Muunilinst]]. He agrees to send a strike force that includes Anakin Skywalker and suggests that Anakin be given "special command" of Obi-Wan Kenobi's fighters. Yoda and Obi-Wan initially speak against the idea but eventually agree.<ref>{{Cite episode|title=Chapter 1|series=Star Wars: Clone Wars|series-link=Star Wars: Clone Wars|network=Cartoon Network|airdate= November 7, 2003|season=1|number=1}}</ref> In the seventh chapter, a holographic image of Sidious appears shortly after Dooku trains the [[Dark Jedi]] [[Asajj Ventress]]. Sidious orders Ventress to track down and kill Anakin. He remarks to Dooku that Ventress is certain to be defeated, but that the purpose of her mission is to test Anakin.<ref>{{Cite episode|title=Chapter 7|series=Star Wars: Clone Wars|series-link=Star Wars: Clone Wars|network=Cartoon Network|airdate= November 17, 2003|season=1|number=7}}</ref> In the final chapters, Sidious orders General Grievous to begin an assault on the galactic capital.<ref>{{Cite episode|title=Chapter 22|series=Star Wars: Clone Wars|series-link=Star Wars: Clone Wars|network=[[Cartoon Network]]|airdate=March 22, 2003|season=3|number=2}}</ref> Later, Palpatine watches as the Separatist invasion of Coruscant begins. Grievous breaks through the Chancellor's window and attempts to kidnap him, leading to a long chase during which Palpatine is protected by the Jedi [[Shaak Ti]], Roron Corobb and Foul Moudama.<ref>{{Cite episode |title=Chapter 23 |series=Star Wars: Clone Wars |series-link=Star Wars: Clone Wars |network=[[Cartoon Network]] |airdate=March 23, 2005 |season=3 |number=3}}</ref> After Grievous defeats the Jedi, Palpatine is taken aboard the ''Invisible Hand'', setting the stage for ''Revenge of the Sith''.<ref>{{Cite episode|title=Chapter 25|series=Star Wars: Clone Wars|series-link=Star Wars: Clone Wars|network=[[Cartoon Network]]|airdate= March 25, 2005|season=3|number=5}}</ref>

=== Books and comics ===
Palpatine made his first major appearance in ''Star Wars''-related comic books in 1991 and 1992, with the ''[[Dark Empire]]'' series written by [[Tom Veitch]] and illustrated by [[Cam Kennedy]]. In the series (set six years after ''Return of the Jedi''), Palpatine is resurrected as the Emperor Reborn, or "Palpatine the Undying". His spirit returns from the netherworld of the Force with the aid of Sith ghosts on [[Korriban]] and possesses the body of [[Jeng Droga]], one of Palpatine's elite spies and assassins known as the Emperor's Hands. Droga flees to a secret Imperial base on the planet [[Byss]], where the Emperor's advisor Sate Pestage [[exorcism|exorcises]] Palpatine's spirit and channels it into one of many clones created by Palpatine before his death. Palpatine attempts to resume control of the galaxy, but Luke Skywalker, now a senior Jedi Knight, sabotages his plans. Luke destroys most of Palpatine's cloning tanks but is only able to defeat the Emperor with help from [[Princess Leia|Leia Organa Solo]], who has received rudimentary Jedi training from Luke. The two repel a Force storm Palpatine had created and turn it back onto him, once again destroying his physical form.<ref>{{cite comic|writer=[[Tom Veitch]]|title=Dark Empire|publisher=[[Dark Horse Comics]]|location=Milwaukie, Oregon|date=1993}}</ref>

Palpatine's ultimate fate is further chronicled in the ''[[Dark Empire II]]'' and ''[[Empire's End]]'' series of comics. The ''Dark Empire II'' series, published from 1994 to 1995, details how the Emperor is once again reborn on Byss into a clone body. Palpatine tries to rebuild the Empire as the Rebel Alliance grows weak.<ref>{{cite comic|writer=Tom Veitch|title=[[Dark Empire II]]|publisher=[[Dark Horse Comics]]|location=Milwaukie, Oregon|date=1995}}</ref> In ''Empire's End'' (1995), a traitorous Imperial guard bribes Palpatine's cloning supervisor to tamper with the Emperor's stored [[DNA]] samples. This causes the clones to deteriorate at a rapid rate. Palpatine tries to possess the body of [[Anakin Solo]], the infant son of Leia Organa and [[Han Solo]], before the clone body dies, but is thwarted once again by Luke Skywalker. Palpatine is killed by a blaster shot fired by Han, but his spirit is captured by the mortally wounded Jedi [[Empatojayos Brand]]. When Brand dies, he takes Palpatine's spirit with him into the netherworld of the Force, destroying the Sith Lord once and for all.<ref>{{cite comic|writer=Tom Veitch|title=[[Empire's End]]|publisher=[[Dark Horse Comics]]|location=Milwaukie, Oregon|date=1997}}</ref>

Novels and comics published before 1999 focus on Palpatine's role as Galactic Emperor. ''[[Star Wars: Shadows of the Empire (novel)|Shadows of the Empire]]'' (1996) by [[Steve Perry (author)|Steve Perry]] and ''[[The Mandalorian Armor]]'' (1998) by [[K. W. Jeter]]—both set between ''The Empire Strikes Back'' and ''Return of the Jedi''—show how Palpatine uses crime lords such as [[Prince Xizor]] and [[bounty hunter]]s like [[Boba Fett]] to fight his enemies.<ref>{{cite book|first=Steve|last=Perry|title=Shadows of the Empire|publisher=Bantam Spectra|location=New York City|date=1996|isbn=978-0-553-57413-5}}</ref><ref>{{cite book|first=K. W.|last=Jeter|title=The Mandalorian Armor|url=https://archive.org/details/starwarsmandalor00jete|url-access=limited|publisher=Bantam Spectra|location=New York City|date=1998|page=[https://archive.org/details/starwarsmandalor00jete/page/144 144]|isbn=978-0-553-57885-0}}</ref> In the [[Jedi Prince series|''Jedi Prince'' series]] of young-reader novels (1992–1993) by [[Paul Davids|Paul and Hollace Davids]], set about a year after ''Return of the Jedi'', a three-eyed [[mutant]] named [[Triclops (Star Wars)|Triclops]] is revealed to be Palpatine's illegitimate son;<ref>{{cite book|last1=Davids|first1=Paul|title=Mission from Mount Yoda|title-link=Mission from Mount Yoda|last2=Davids|first2=Hollace|date=1993|publisher=[[Scholastic Books]]|isbn=978-0-553-15890-8|location=New York City}}</ref> he had a son named Ken, the titular "Jedi Prince". [[Barbara Hambly]]'s novel ''[[Children of the Jedi]]'' (1995), set eight years after ''Return of the Jedi'', features a woman named [[Roganda Ismaren]] who claims that Palpatine fathered her son [[Irek Ismaren|Irek]].<ref>{{cite book|first=Barbara|last=Hambly|title=Children of the Jedi|publisher=[[Bantam Spectra]]|location=New York City|date=1996|isbn=978-0-553-57293-3|title-link=Children of the Jedi}}</ref>

After the release of ''The Phantom Menace'', writers were allowed to begin exploring Palpatine's backstory as a politician and Sith lord. The comic "Marked" by [[Rob Williams (comics)|Rob Williams]], printed in ''[[Star Wars Tales 24]]'' (2005), and [[Michael Reaves]]' novel ''[[Darth Maul: Shadow Hunter]]'' (2001) explain Darth Sidious' relationship with his apprentice Darth Maul.<ref>{{cite comic|writer=Rob Williams|story=Marked|title=[[Star Wars Tales]]|volume=24|publisher=[[Dark Horse Comics]]|location=Milwaukie, Oregon|date=July 2005}}</ref><ref>{{cite book|first=Michael|last=Reaves|author-link=Michael Reaves|title=Darth Maul: Shadow Hunter|publisher=[[Del Rey Books]]|location=New York City|year=2001|isbn=978-0-345-43541-5|title-link=Darth Maul: Shadow Hunter}}</ref> ''[[Cloak of Deception]]'' (2001) by [[James Luceno]] follows Reaves' novel and details how Darth Sidious encourages the Trade Federation to build an army of battle droids in preparation for the invasion of Naboo. ''Cloak of Deception'' also focuses on Palpatine's early political career, revealing how he becomes a confidant of Chancellor Finis Valorum and acquainted with Padmé Amidala, newly elected queen of Naboo.<ref>{{cite book|first=James|last=Luceno|author-link=James Luceno|title=Cloak of Deception|publisher=[[Del Rey Books]]|location=New York City|date=2001|isbn=978-0-345-44297-0}}</ref> Palpatine's role during the Clone Wars as Chancellor of the Republic and Darth Sidious is portrayed in novels such as [[Matthew Stover]]'s ''[[Shatterpoint]]'' (2003), [[Steven Barnes]]' ''[[The Cestus Deception]]'' (2004), [[Sean Stewart]]'s ''[[Yoda: Dark Rendezvous]]'' (2004), and Luceno's ''[[Labyrinth of Evil]]'' (2005) and ''[[Darth Plagueis]]'' (2012).

Following the theatrical release of ''Revenge of the Sith'', ''Star Wars'' literature focused on Palpatine's role after the creation of the Empire. [[John Ostrander]]'s comic ''Star Wars Republic 78: Loyalties'' (2005) chronicles how, shortly after seizing power, Emperor Palpatine sends Darth Vader to assassinate Sagoro Autem, an Imperial captain who plans to defect from the Empire.<ref>{{cite comic|writer=[[John Ostrander]]|story=Loyalties|title=[[Star Wars: Republic]]|issue=78|publisher=[[Dark Horse Comics]]|location=Milwaukie, Oregon|date=October 2005}}</ref> In Luceno's novel ''[[Dark Lord: The Rise of Darth Vader]]'' (2005) (set shortly after ''Revenge of the Sith''), the Emperor sends Darth Vader to the planet Murkhana to discover why clone troopers there refused to carry out [[Great Jedi Purge|Order 66]] against their Jedi generals. Palpatine hopes these early missions will teach Vader what it means to be a Sith and crush any remnants of Anakin Skywalker.<ref>{{cite book|first=James|last=Luceno|author-link=James Luceno|title=Dark Lord: The Rise of Darth Vader|publisher=[[Del Rey Books]]|location=New York City|date=2005|isbn=978-0-345-47733-0|title-link=Dark Lord: The Rise of Darth Vader}}</ref>

[[James Luceno]]'s 2012 novel ''[[Darth Plagueis]]'' depicts Palpatine's early life, prior to the films. The scion of an aristocratic family on Naboo, Palpatine first turns toward the dark side upon meeting the titular Sith lord. Sensing great power in Palpatine, Plageuis takes him on as his Sith apprentice. In the final test of his devotion to the dark side, Palpatine kills his parents and his brother and sister.<ref>{{cite web|first=Thomas|last=Bacon|url=https://screenrant.com/star-wars-rise-skywalker-emperor-palpatine-backstory-return-explained/|title=Emperor Palpatine's Entire Backstory, Timeline, & Manipulations Explained|website=[[Screen Rant]]|date=December 25, 2019|access-date=May 26, 2020|archive-date=August 6, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806115535/https://screenrant.com/star-wars-rise-skywalker-emperor-palpatine-backstory-return-explained/|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Video games ===
In ''[[Star Wars: The Force Unleashed|The Force Unleashed]],'' the Empire has captured the rebel leaders and holds them on the Death Star. Vader's former apprentice Galen Marek/Starkiller battles his way through the station, defeats Vader and faces the Emperor, who tries to goad him into killing Vader so Starkiller can take his place. Starkiller defeats the Emperor, but spares him. The Emperor then unleashes Force lightning at Starkiller's companion [[List of Star Wars Legends characters#Rahm Kota|Rahm Kota]], but Starkiller absorbs it, sacrificing himself to allow the rebels to escape.

In the noncanonical dark side ending Starkiller kills Vader and is congratulated by the Emperor, who commands him to kill Kota. Starkiller instead attacks the Emperor, who foils his attempt and then crushes him and the rebels with a ship. Starkiller later awakens to find his body being grafted with armor to continue serving the Emperor.

In the downloadable content for ''[[Star Wars: The Force Unleashed II|The Force Unleashed II]]'', the Rebel Alliance gets defeated at the Battle of Endor. The Emperor then subdues Vader with Force lightning while chastising him for resurrecting his failed apprentice as a clone.

== Characterization ==
According to the prologue of Alan Dean Foster's 1976 novelization of the original ''Star Wars'' film,
{{blockquote|Aided and abetted by restless, power-hungry individuals within the government, and the massive organs of commerce, the ambitious Senator Palpatine caused himself to be elected President of the Republic. He promised to reunite the disaffected among the people and to restore the remembered glory of the Republic. Once secure in office he declared himself Emperor, shutting himself away from the populace. Soon he was controlled by the very assistants and boot-lickers he had appointed to high office, and the cries of the people for justice did not reach his ears.<ref name="Lucas-1976"/>}}

In ''Cloak of Deception'', Luceno writes that Palpatine carefully guards his privacy and "others found his reclusiveness intriguing, as if he led a secret life".<ref name="Luceno-2002">{{cite book |last=Luceno |first=James |title=Cloak of Deception |title-link=Cloak of Deception |date=2002 |publisher=[[Del Ray Books]] |isbn=978-0345442970 |location=New York City |page=124 |author-link=James Luceno}}</ref> Despite this, he has many allies in the government. Luceno writes, "What Palpatine lacked in charisma, he made up for in candor, and it was that directness that had led to his widespread appeal in the senate. ... For in his heart he judged the universe on his own terms, with a clear sense of right and wrong."<ref name="Luceno-2002" /> In [[Terry Brooks]]' [[Star Wars: Episode I – The Phantom Menace (novel)|novelization of ''The Phantom Menace'']], Palpatine claims to embrace democratic principles. He tells Queen Amidala, "I promise, Your Majesty, if I am elected [chancellor of the Republic], I will restore democracy to the Republic. I will put an end to the corruption that has plagued the Senate."<ref>{{cite book |last=Brooks |first=Terry |title=Star Wars Episode I: The Phantom Menace |title-link=Star Wars Episode I: The Phantom Menace (novel) |date=1999 |publisher=[[Del Rey Books]] |isbn=978-0-345-43411-1 |location=New York City |pages=[https://archive.org/details/phantommenace00broo_0/page/238 238–239] |author-link=Terry Brooks}}</ref> A ''Visual Dictionary'' states that he is a self-proclaimed savior.<ref>{{cite encyclopedia |year=2005 |title=The Visual Dictionary of Star Wars, Episode III Revenge of the Sith |publisher=Dorling Kindersley Publishing, Inc. |location=London, England |last=Luceno |first=James |author-link=James Luceno |page=100 |isbn=978-0-7566-1128-6}}</ref> According to the ''[[Star Wars Encyclopedia]]'', "[Palpatine's] Empire ... is based on tyranny."<ref>{{cite encyclopedia |year=1998 |title=Emperor Palpatine |encyclopedia=[[Star Wars Encyclopedia]] |publisher=[[Del Ray Books]] |location=New York City |url=https://archive.org/details/starwarsencyclop00sans/page/224 |editor-last=Sansweet |editor-first=Stephen J. |editor-link=Stephen J. Sansweet |page=[https://archive.org/details/starwarsencyclop00sans/page/224 224] |isbn=978-0-345-40227-1}}</ref>

Expanded Universe materials explicitly establish that Palpatine was the apprentice of Darth Plagueis.<ref>{{cite web |title=Darth Sidious |url=https://www.starwars.com/databank/character/darthsidious/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061205030541/http://starwars.com/databank/character/darthsidious |archive-date=December 5, 2006 |access-date=August 17, 2006 |website=Star Wars Databank}}</ref> Palpatine is characterized as "the most powerful practitioner of the Sith ways in modern times."<ref>{{cite web |title=Palpatine, Expanded Universe |url=https://www.starwars.com/databank/character/palpatine/?id=eu |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061201010639/http://www.starwars.com/databank/character/palpatine/?id=eu |archive-date=December 1, 2006 |access-date=August 17, 2006 |website=Star Wars Databank}}</ref> Palpatine is so powerful that he is able to mask his true identity from the Jedi for decades. In the novel ''[[Shatterpoint]]'', Mace Windu remarks to Yoda, "A shame [Palpatine] can't touch the Force. He might have been a fine Jedi."<ref>{{cite book |last=Stover |first=Matthew |title=Shatterpoint |title-link=Shatterpoint |date=2003 |publisher=[[Del Rey Books]] |isbn=978-0-345-45574-1 |location=New York City |page=[https://archive.org/details/shatterpointstar00matt/page/10 10] |author-link=Matthew Stover}}</ref>

The ''Star Wars'' Databank explains that the Force "granted him inhuman dexterity and speed, agility enough to quickly kill three Jedi Masters" (as depicted in ''Revenge of the Sith'').<ref name="Star Wars Databank-2" /> Stover describes the duel between Yoda and Palpatine in his [[Star Wars: Episode III – Revenge of the Sith (novel)|novelization of ''Revenge of the Sith'']] thus: "From the shadow of a black wing, a small weapon&nbsp;... slid into a withered hand and spat a flame-colored blade ... When those blades met, it was more than Yoda against Palpatine, more the millennia of Sith against the legions of Jedi; this was the expression of the fundamental conflict of the universe itself. Light against dark. Winner takes all."<ref>{{cite book |last=Stover |first=Matthew |title=Revenge of the Sith |title-link=Star Wars Episode III: Revenge of the Sith (novel) |date=2005 |publisher=[[Del Ray Books]] |isbn=978-0345428844 |location=New York City |page=[https://archive.org/details/revengeofsith00stov/page/424 424] |author-link=Matthew Stover}}</ref> During the duel, Yoda realizes that Sidious is a superior warrior, and represents a small but powerful Sith Order that had changed and evolved over the years, while the Jedi had not: "He had lost before he started. He had lost before he was born."<ref>Stover, page 426</ref>

According to the Databank and ''New Essential Guide to Characters'', Palpatine possesses great patience and his maneuverings are as a [[dejarik]] grandmaster moves pieces on a board.<ref>{{cite encyclopedia |year=2002 |title=The New Essential Guide to Characters |entry=Emperor Palpatine |publisher=[[Del Ray Books]] |location=New York City |isbn=978-0-345-44900-9 |last2=Sutfin |first2=Michael |first1=Daniel |last1=Wallace}}</ref> He is depicted as a diabolical genius.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Slavicsek |first1=Bill |title=Revised Core Rulebook (Star Wars Roleplaying Game) |last2=Collins |first2=Andy |last3=Wiker |first3=J.D. |last4=Sansweet |first4=Steven J. |date=2002 |publisher=[[Wizards of the Coast]] |isbn=978-0-7869-2876-7 |location=Renton, Washington}}</ref><ref>[http://www.sideshowtoy.com/behindtheshow/?page_id=1389 Movie, Television and Proprietary Collectible Figures - Sideshow Collectibles, Inc. - Toy, WETA, Lord of the Rings, James Bond, Muppets, Military, Universal Monsters<!-- Bot generated title -->] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080122132846/http://www.sideshowtoy.com/behindtheshow/?page_id=1389|date=2008-01-22}}</ref>

==In popular culture==
[[File:Palpatine Unnatural (49867763757).jpg|thumb|An [[Oregon Department of Transportation]] [[public safety announcement|PSA]] parodying Palpatine]]
With the premiere of ''Return of the Jedi'' and the prequel films and the accompanying merchandising campaign, Palpatine became an icon in American popular culture. Academics have debated the relationship of Palpatine to modern culture. Religion scholars Ross Shepard Kraemer, William Cassidy, and Susan Schwartz compare Palpatine and ''Star Wars'' heroes to the [[theology|theological]] concept of [[Dualistic cosmology#Moral dualism|dualism]]. They insist, "One can certainly picture the evil emperor in ''Star Wars'' as [[Satan]], complete with his infernal powers, leading his faceless minions such as his red-robed Imperial Guards."<ref>{{cite book|first1=Ross|last1=Shepard Kraemer|first2=William|last2=Cassidy|first3=Susan|last3=Schwartz|title=Religions of Star Trek|publisher=[[Westview Press]]|location=Boulder, Colorado|date=2003|page=69|isbn=978-0-8133-4115-6}}</ref> Lawrence and Jewett argue that Vader killing Palpatine in ''Return of the Jedi'' represented "the permanent subduing of evil".<ref>{{cite book |last1=Shelton Lawrence |first1=John |url=https://archive.org/details/mythofamericansu00lawr_0/page/266 |title=The Myth of the American Superhero |last2=Jewett |first2=Robert |date=2002 |publisher=[[Wm. B. Eerdmans Publishing]] |isbn=978-0-8028-4911-3 |location=Grand Rapids, Michigan |page=[https://archive.org/details/mythofamericansu00lawr_0/page/266 266]}}</ref> However, according to director J. J. Abrams, Palpatine's return in ''The Rise of Skywalker'' represented the idea that evil can return as a result of complacency.<ref name="Bonomolo-2019">{{cite web |url=https://comicbook.com/starwars/news/star-wars-the-rise-of-skywalker-jj-abrams-explains-twist-emperor-palpatine-legacy-lineage-rey-palpatine/ |title=''Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker'' Director J.J. Abrams Explains Twist Behind Emperor Palpatine's Legacy |website=Comicbook.com |date=December 21, 2019 |access-date=September 18, 2020 |first=Cameron |last=Bonomolo |archive-date=April 10, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210410200845/https://comicbook.com/starwars/news/star-wars-the-rise-of-skywalker-jj-abrams-explains-twist-emperor-palpatine-legacy-lineage-rey-palpatine/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He said, "I think the idea that if we are not careful, the evil — the ultimate evil — will rise again."<ref name="Bonomolo-2019"/>

Since ''Return of the Jedi'' and the prequel films, Palpatine's name has been invoked as a caricature in politics. A ''[[Seattle Post-Intelligencer]]'' editorial noted that anti-[[Pork barrel|pork]] bloggers were caricaturing West Virginia Senator [[Robert Byrd]] as "the Emperor Palpatine of pork", with Senator [[Ted Stevens]] of Alaska having "clear aspirations to be his Darth Vader." The charge followed a report that linked a [[secret hold]] on the [[Federal Funding Accountability and Transparency Act of 2006]] to the two senators.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.seattlepi.com/opinion/283392_secreted.html|title=Open Government: Pork protection|work=Seattle Post-Intelligencer|location=Seattle, Washington|date=September 1, 2006|access-date=September 5, 2006}}</ref> Politicians have made comparisons as well. In 2005, [[U.S. Democratic Party|Democratic]] Senator [[Frank Lautenberg]] of New Jersey compared [[U.S. Republican Party|Republican]] [[Party leaders of the United States Senate|Majority Leader]] [[Bill Frist]] of Tennessee to Palpatine in a speech on the Senate floor, complete with a visual aid.<ref>{{cite news|first=Alec|last=Russell|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/worldnews/northamerica/usa/1490425/Star-Wars-jibe-in-US-Senate-battle-of-the-filibuster.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220111/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/worldnews/northamerica/usa/1490425/Star-Wars-jibe-in-US-Senate-battle-of-the-filibuster.html |archive-date=January 11, 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live|title=Star Wars jibe in US Senate battle of the filibuster|newspaper=[[The Guardian]]|location=London, England|date=20 May 2005|access-date=4 May 2019}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

A [[Fox News]] editorial stated "no cultural icon can exist without someone trying to stuff it into a political ideology. The ''Star Wars'' saga, the greatest [[popular culture|pop culture icon]] of the last three decades, is no exception... Palpatine's dissolution of the Senate in favor of imperial rule has been compared to Julius Caesar's marginalization of the [[Roman Senate]], Hitler's power-grabs as chancellor on his way to becoming a dictator, [[Franklin D. Roosevelt]]'s failed [[Judiciary Reorganization Bill of 1937|court-packing scheme]] and the creation of a perceived [[Imperial Presidency]] in the United States."<ref>{{cite news |last=Firey |first=Thomas A. |date=May 22, 2005 |title=Star Wars Saga Reflects Political Ideals |url=http://www.foxnews.com/story/0,2933,157229,00.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060505171623/http://www.foxnews.com/story/0,2933,157229,00.html |archive-date=May 5, 2006 |access-date=August 17, 2006 |publisher=[[Fox News Channel]] |location=New York}}</ref>

===On the Internet===
In the mid-late 2010s, Palpatine became the subject of various [[internet meme]]s, which referenced certain lines of dialogue he spoke in ''Revenge of the Sith''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.dailydot.com/unclick/darth-plagueis-palpatine-senate-meme/|title=Suddenly, Star Wars' Chancellor Palpatine has conquered the entire meme landscape|last=Hathaway|first=Jay|date=January 27, 2017|work=[[Daily Dot]]|access-date=April 14, 2019|archive-date=December 13, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211213230639/https://www.dailydot.com/unclick/darth-plagueis-palpatine-senate-meme/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://melmagazine.com/en-us/story/the-reddit-group-dedicated-to-making-memes-about-the-star-wars-prequels|title=The Reddit Group Dedicated to Making Memes About the 'Star Wars' Prequels|last=McDermott|first=John|work=[[Mel Magazine]]|date=February 13, 2018|access-date=April 14, 2019|archive-date=April 13, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190413143004/https://melmagazine.com/en-us/story/the-reddit-group-dedicated-to-making-memes-about-the-star-wars-prequels|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.vice.com/en/article/reddit-is-engaged-in-a-highly-entertaining-star-wars-meme-war/|title=Reddit Is Engaged in a Highly Entertaining 'Star Wars' Meme War|last=Koebler|first=Jason|date=March 14, 2017|work=[[Vice (magazine)|Motherboard (website)]]|access-date=April 14, 2019|archive-date=April 13, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190413143003/https://motherboard.vice.com/en_us/article/mg49gx/reddit-is-engaged-in-a-highly-entertaining-star-wars-meme-war|url-status=live}}</ref>

Most notably, a screenshot of the character in ''Revenge of the Sith'' was posted to [[Reddit]] in 2017 and became the most upvoted post in the history of that website for the next three years, amassing over 438 thousand upvotes as of 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Keeley |first=Joe |date=2020-03-31 |title=The Top 10 Highest-Rated Reddit Posts of All Time |url=https://www.makeuseof.com/tag/10-top-rated-reddit-posts-time/ |access-date=2025-01-05 |website=MUO |language=en}}</ref>  The post's caption reads: "The Senate. Upvote this so that people see it when they Google 'The Senate'."{{efn|In reference to a line spoken by Palpatine in the same movie: "I am the Senate!"}}

